{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01X5h7PVZL3BLhMMGo99SWzy","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# Paper Framing — irt_equated_bench_regen\n2\t\n3\t## Target Venue\n4\t- Primary: EMNLP 2026 (via ARR; long paper 8-page limit + unlimited refs)\n5\t- Page limit: 8 pages main text (当前 10 页，需压缩 2 页)\n6\t\n7\t## Paper Type\n8\tEmpirical analysis / psychometric characterization. Discovery paper, not methods paper. \"DIF reveals benchmark structural problems,\" not \"DIF detects contamination.\"\n9\t\n10\t## Core Claims (4 条, 可证伪)\n11\t\n12\t**Claim 1 (Temporal Comparability Concern)**: ~31% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations — they exhibit large DIF (ETS C, |Δ_MH|≥1.5) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts. Primary estimate: **31.3%** (temporal cohort, plan_001). All 57 subjects show instability (C% range 20.7–48.4%, unweighted mean ≈30%, plan_001 domain_specificity.csv). Within-subject matching on 5 high-C% subjects yields higher rates (31-45%, avg 38.1%, plan_014) — report as robustness check, not primary. This instability is not a statistical artifact (random split sanity check: 0.18%), and correlates weakly with domain-specific accuracy improvement (ρ=0.307, p=0.02), but item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains only 2.4pp (7.6%). Architecture heterogeneity is a non-issue: METABENCH MMLU is 99.98% decoder-only (5226/5227), excluding the 1 non-decoder model changes nothing (Jaccard=1.0). Threshold sensitivity robustness confirmed: C% ranges from 48.9% (|Δ_MH|≥1.0) to 18.3% (|Δ_MH|≥2.0) to 10.2% (|Δ_MH|≥2.5). BH FDR correction at q=0.05 has negligible impact (removes 3/3,918 items).\n13\t- Evidence: plan_001 (C%=31.3%), plan_014 (within-subject C%=38.1%), plan_010 (arch-stratified: 99.98% decoder-only, C% unchanged, Jaccard=1.0), plan_018 (threshold: 49/31/18/10% at 1.0/1.5/2.0/2.5; BH negligible), plan_019 (decomposition: ≤2.4pp/7.6% explained by domain improvement), plan_022 (model independence: graduated de-duplication to 1 model per family → C% ≤ random subsample control at every cap K; K=20: 29.1% vs random 30.9%, K=5: 11.7% vs random 16.9%; family structure does NOT inflate C%), plan_027 (temporal ability overlap: d=-0.17, OVL=76.4%; 1:1 NN matching C%=28.9%±0.1% vs full 31.3% — ability confound explains only 2.4pp; contrast with Llama-2v3 d=-0.99→matched C%=28.3%, where ability confound explains 33pp)\n14\t- Honesty requirement: ρ=0.307 (p=0.02) at subject level reflects a modest ecological correlation. Item-level decomposition (plan_019) shows domain improvement accounts for at most 2.4pp of 31.3% C% (7.6%; pseudo R²=0.0007). The instability is predominantly item-specific. Caveat: item difficulty may be a mediator rather than confounder — use Model 1 as primary; Model 2 (Simpson's paradox) as supplementary.\n15\t- IRT fit: 98% of models show |Lz|>2, reflecting MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (57 subjects), not architecture heterogeneity. 1D IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU — discuss in limitations as motivation for future multidimensional IRT work.\n16\t\n17\t**Claim 2 (Ground Truth Meta-critique)**: Existing contamination detection literature validates against web-overlap/n-gram labels that measure global exposure (whether an item is publicly accessible), not differential contamination (whether an item functions differently across model cohorts). DIF enrichment≈1.0 against Li et al. labels is expected, not failure — these labels cannot evaluate item-level differential detectors. This is a fundamental validation methodology gap: all work using web-overlap labels as ground truth for item-level detectors (including Min-K%, n-gram methods) has the same blind spot. **Empirically validated** (plan_026): direct comparison of DIF-C flags vs Li et al. web-overlap labels on 9,229 MMLU items shows enrichment=0.964, Jaccard=0.206, Phi=0.012, χ²=1.28 (p=0.258), OR=1.05 [0.96, 1.15]. Consistent across subtypes (input_only: 0.986, input_and_label: 0.960). Semi-synthetic injection confirms DIF can detect differential contamination (ΔTPR=+0.508 at p=0.5, dose-response from p=0.3 to p=1.0).\n18\t- Evidence: plan_001 (enrichment=0.96), plan_026 (direct comparison: enrichment=0.964, Jaccard=0.206, p=0.258), plan_011 (ΔTPR=+0.508, dose-response)\n19\t- Framing: aggressive — \"the field's standard validation approach is fundamentally mismatched for item-level detection\" — now with direct empirical evidence, not just conceptual argument\n20\t\n21\t**Claim 3 (Semi-synthetic Validation Framework)**: The ΔTPR methodology — injecting known differential contamination into real response matrices and measuring detection power increase — provides a controlled validation infrastructure for any item-level contamination detector. Cross-subject matching (using total score on OTHER subjects as MH matching variable) eliminates FPR inflation (ΔFPR=0.000 across all 5 tested subjects), while within-subject matching suffers from matching variable contamination (FPR inflation +0.186).\n22\t- Evidence: plan_011, plan_016 (ΔFPR=0.000 cross-subject, 4/5 subjects ΔTPR>0.3), plan_004 Step 2 (GSM8K ΔTPR=+0.818 standard, +0.517 external)\n23\t- Cross-benchmark: GSM8K replication confirms framework portability to single-domain math benchmarks\n24\t- Positioning: cross-subject matching is a practical methodological recommendation; cross-benchmark replication strengthens generalizability\n25\t\n26\t**Claim 4 (Downstream Impact — Four-Layer Finding)**: DIF as diagnostic lens, not quality filter. Four sub-findings:\n27\t1. **Benchmark redundancy**: ρ≈0.997 between full and stable-only rankings — MMLU rankings are extremely robust to 31% item removal, revealing massive measurement redundancy in the benchmark.\n28\t2. **DIF items carry cross-cohort signal but are NOT the most ranking-defining items**: DIF removal causes MORE disruption than random removal (z=-8 to -49σ, 21/21 conditions), confirming DIF items are non-trivial. But circularity ablation (plan_020) shows DIF is the LEAST disruptive non-random strategy: accuracy-gap removal disrupts 10-16× more (z=-157 vs -9.5), high-variance 11× more (z=-108), high-discrimination 1.7× more (z=-16). MH's ability conditioning filters out raw accuracy differences, isolating genuine differential functioning rather than generally informative items. DIF-discrimination overlap is only 22.1% (Jaccard=0.12) — DIF identifies \"items that changed across generations,\" not \"good items.\"\n29\t3. **DIF as diagnostic lens**: DIF identifies which items' functioning changes most across model generations → reveals which capability dimensions drive ranking shifts. Not \"find bad items and remove\" but \"understand what changed and why.\" Model-specific patterns: phi-3/llama-3 gain +700-850 ranks from DIF items, mistral merges lose -400 to -587. **Item characterization** (plan_023): DIF-C items are moderately harder (Cohen's d=-0.247) and higher-discrimination (d=0.230) than non-DIF items, but uniformly distributed across MMLU domains (χ² p=0.32). Key finding: difficulty × DIF direction interaction (ρ=-0.597) — hardest-decile DIF-C items are 88.7% focal-favoring (2024 models do better), easiest-decile are 96.2% reference-favoring (2023 models do better). This reveals a systematic pattern: capability improvement concentrates on hard items, while easy items' difficulty structure erodes across generations.\n30\t4. **Cross-benchmark validation — DIF items resist contamination-based gains**: On GSM8K (1,319 items × 6,702 models), DIF flags anti-correlate with GSM1K contamination gap (r=−0.478, p=0.005, 95% BCa CI [−0.714, −0.155]; Spearman ρ=−0.403, p=0.020, CI [−0.687, −0.031]; partial r=−0.385 controlling GSM8K accuracy, p=0.030, CI [−0.640, −0.058]; permutation p=0.014). All CIs exclude zero. Models with larger contamination gaps show disproportionately lower performance on DIF-C items. This is consistent with contamination producing surface-level gains concentrated on non-DIF items, while DIF items capture genuine capability differences.\n31\t- Evidence: plan_017 (z=-8 to -49σ, ρ=0.996-0.999), plan_020 (circularity ablation: DIF z=-9.5 vs acc-gap z=-157, disc z=-16, var z=-108), plan_004 Step 3 (r=−0.478, p=0.005, N=33), plan_023 (item characterization: difficulty ρ=-0.597, domain uniformity χ² p=0.32)\n32\t- Architecture: plan_017 is load-bearing (strong, full N=5,227), Step 3 adds contamination dimension but is suggestive (N=33, CI wide). If Step 3 attacked, plan_017 stands independently.\n33\t- Growing Pains互补: \"Growing Pains uses anchor items for efficient evaluation; our DIF diagnostic tells them which anchor items are degrading\"\n34\t- Caveats (→ limitations): N=33, Pearson CI upper bound |r|=0.155 (small effect possible), Spearman CI upper bound |ρ|=0.031 (near zero possible); item difficulty confound not fully excluded (DIF-C items harder for all models); contamination gap is proxy, not ground truth; replication with larger matched sample needed\n35\t\n36\t## Story Spine (v2 — no-pivot narrative)\n37\t\n38\t**Opening frame**: LLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability — when we say \"Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,\" we presume both scores reflect the same latent construct. But do they? Psychometrics has a mature tool for testing exactly this: Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability. We apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items) — the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\n39\t\n40\t**Key narrative principle**: \"Instability ≠ invalidity.\" DIF flags items whose functioning changed across generations — this is a diagnostic signal, not a quality verdict. MMLU rankings are massively redundant (ρ≈0.997 after removing 31% of items), so instability doesn't threaten validity. But it reveals WHICH capability dimensions are shifting. **This framing must appear in the Introduction, not be deferred to Discussion.**\n41\t\n42\t**What we find** (4 contributions, focused):\n43\t\n44\t1. **31% of MMLU items are functionally unstable** across model generations (ETS C, |Δ_MH|≥1.5). Robust to threshold choice (49%/31%/18%/10% at 1.0/1.5/2.0/2.5), multiple testing (BH removes 3/3918 items), and architecture composition (99.98% decoder-only). Domain accuracy improvement explains ≤2.4pp of 31.3% C% (7.6%; pseudo R²=0.0007). The instability is predominantly item-specific, not a proxy for \"models got better.\"\n45\t\n46\t2. **Ground truth meta-critique**: Web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination. Enrichment≈1.0 is expected. Semi-synthetic injection confirms DIF detects differential contamination (ΔTPR=+0.508). Any item-level detector validated against web-overlap labels has this blind spot.\n47\t\n48\t3. **Semi-synthetic validation framework**: ΔTPR methodology with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000). Portable across benchmarks (GSM8K ΔTPR=+0.818). Reusable for validating any item-level contamination detector.\n49\t\n50\t4. **DIF as diagnostic lens**: DIF items carry cross-cohort signal (z=-8 to -49σ vs random) but are the LEAST disruptive non-random strategy (z=-9.5 vs accuracy-gap z=-157). DIF isolates genuine differential functioning, not just informative items. Cross-benchmark: DIF items resist contamination-based gains (r=−0.478, BCa CI [−0.71, −0.16]).\n51\t\n52\t**Headline result**: \"Psychometric DIF analysis of 5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items reveals 31% temporal instability — robust across thresholds, not driven by capability improvement (≤7.6%), and not a validity threat (ρ≈0.997 ranking stability). The field's web-overlap ground truth cannot capture this differential signal; we provide a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000). DIF items resist contamination-based gains (r=−0.478) and serve as a diagnostic lens for understanding what changed across model generations.\"\n53\t\n54\t## Scope Control (main body vs appendix)\n55\t\n56\t**MAIN BODY (4 claims + robustness)**:\n57\t- §1 Introduction (problem + \"instability ≠ invalidity\" framing upfront)\n58\t- §2 Related Work (45 papers, 5 clusters)\n59\t- §3 Method (MH-DIF primer, semi-synthetic framework, cross-subject matching)\n60\t- §4 Experiments: Claim 1 (temporal DIF + threshold + BH + decomposition + architecture), Claim 2 (enrichment + meta-critique), Claim 3 (semi-synthetic + cross-benchmark), Claim 4 (redundancy + circularity ablation + diagnostic lens + GSM1K validation)\n61\t- §5 Discussion + Limitations\n62\t- §6 Conclusion\n63\t\n64\t**APPENDIX (pruned from main body)**:\n65\t- Bayesian cumulative DIF (plan_007) — simulation-only, underdeveloped\n66\t- RL robustness (plan_002) — N=13, underpowered\n67\t- Ensemble fusion (plan_003a/b/c) — negative, not executed\n68\t- Bifactor equating pilot — below target fit\n69\t- Item generation pilot — below benchmarks\n70\t- Graceful degradation — clean but supplementary\n71\t- Item-Focused Trees — robustness check, supplementary\n72\t- ConStat/DVD comparisons — complementary baselines\n73\t\n74\t## Empirical Observation: Structural Constraints on DIF in LLM Benchmarks\n75\t\n76\tSystematic experimentation reveals that three structural improvements — matched-ability cohort design (plan_001b), IRT θ-conditioning (plan_012, r=0.9986), and bifactor pre-check (plan_014, within-subject C%=38.1%) — all fail to reduce temporal DIF baseline FPR below 31% while maintaining detection power. This is an empirical observation, not a formal impossibility result. The constraints arise from: (a) unidimensional IRT limitations in modeling 57-subject multidimensional ability, (b) statistical power requirements (N≥80, δ≥1.4), and (c) inability to separate ability confounding from genuine item-level changes. Higher-level methods (MIMIC, propensity-score matching, multidimensional IRT) may address individual constraints — future work.\n77\t\n78\t## Venue Fit\n79\t\n80\t- **EMNLP 2026**: Strong fit — NLP evaluation methodology is a dedicated area, reviewers expect empirical studies of benchmarks. \"Empirical, not algorithmic\" contribution type well-received. Risk: 8-page limit requires significant compression (current 10 pages). Mitigation: move structural analysis details to appendix.\n81\t\n82\t## Novelty Articulation (三层，显式化)\n83\t\n84\tMH-DIF (1959/1988) is applied without modification. **Explicitly acknowledge this.** Novelty is NOT algorithmic. Three layers of genuine contribution:\n85\t\n86\t1. **Empirical discovery at unprecedented scale**: First DIF analysis on 5,227×12,508 LLM response matrix. Finding: 31% temporal instability is robust across thresholds (10-49%), not explained by capability improvement (≤7.6%), and architecturally homogeneous (99.98% decoder-only). No prior work has characterized item-level stability of LLM benchmarks at this scale. The instability finding itself is new data about MMLU's measurement properties.\n87\t\n88\t2. **Ground truth meta-critique with field-wide implications**: The observation that web-overlap labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors is NOT obvious — the field routinely uses them. This affects the validity claims of Min-K%, ConStat, n-gram methods, and all papers using web-overlap as ground truth. First empirical demonstration (enrichment≈1.0 + semi-synthetic proof).\n89\t\n90\t3. **Semi-synthetic validation framework + cross-subject matching**: The ΔTPR methodology is a reusable infrastructure contribution. Cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000) is a novel practical innovation — the insight that within-subject matching contaminates the conditioning variable is subtle and general. Portable to any multi-domain benchmark (demonstrated on GSM8K).\n91\t\n92\t**Framing**: \"We bring mature psychometric tools to characterize an urgent ML evaluation problem. The contribution is the empirical findings, the validation methodology critique, and the reusable framework — not a new algorithm.\"\n93\t\n94\t## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns (updated for pivot)\n95\t\n96\t1. **\"31% could just mean models got better / ability confound inflates C%\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_019 + plan_027). Item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains ≤2.4pp of 31.3% C% (7.6%; pseudo R²=0.0007). Temporal cohort ability gap is negligible (d=-0.17, OVL=76.4%); 1:1 NN matching (d≈-0.01) yields C%=28.9%±0.1% vs full 31.3% — ability confound explains only 2.4pp. Contrast: Llama-2v3 (d=-0.99) drops from 61.3% to 28.3% under matching. Both converge to ~28-29% genuine item-level DIF.\n97\t\n98\t2. **\"Ground truth critique is obvious — of course web labels don't capture differential effects\"** — Not obvious: the field routinely uses these labels for item-level validation. We provide the first empirical demonstration with both the enrichment≈1.0 result and the semi-synthetic proof of concept.\n99\t\n100\t3. **\"Semi-synthetic injection is artificial\"** — We inject into real response matrices (not simulated), preserving real ability distributions and item characteristics. The mechanism (incorrect→correct flipping) is a simplification acknowledged in limitations.\n101\t\n102\t4. **\"DIF power is only sufficient for large effects\"** — Large effects are precisely what threaten benchmark validity most. Operational region: N≥80, δ≥1.4, TPR=0.68-0.80.\n103\t\n104\t5. **\"Only validated on MMLU\"** — Partially addressed: GSM8K replication (METABENCH 1,319 items × 6,702 models) confirms semi-synthetic framework portability (ΔTPR=+0.818) and temporal DIF pattern (C%=18.9%). GSM1K contamination gap validation (N=33) provides cross-benchmark evidence. Full generalization to diverse benchmarks remains future work.\n105\t\n106\t6. **\"Higher methods (MIMIC, propensity-score) could solve the confounding\"** — Acknowledged as future work. We frame our three-attempt negative results as \"empirical observation\" that motivates these directions, not as impossibility.\n107\t\n108\t7. **\"ETS threshold is uncalibrated for LLM populations\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_018). C% at {1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5} = {48.9%, 31.3%, 18.3%, 10.2%}. Finding >15% holds at standard thresholds [1.0, 2.0]. At conservative 2.5, still 10.2% (1,278 items). Present as: \"finding is robust across standard thresholds; even at the most conservative cutoff, >10% of items show large DIF.\" Do NOT claim \">15% at all thresholds\" — be honest that 2.5 drops below.\n109\t\n110\t8. **\"Multiple testing: 12,508 simultaneous tests without correction\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_018). BH FDR correction at q=0.05 removes only 3/3,918 ETS C items (31.32%→31.30%). At higher thresholds, BH impact is zero. ETS dual requirement (effect size + significance) provides near-complete implicit protection. Random-split sanity check (C%=0.18%) confirms empirically.\n111\t\n112\t9. **\"Only uniform DIF — non-uniform DIF plausible for LLMs\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_021). LR-DIF on 9 subjects (1787 items): MH C-class 92.1% concordance with LR uniform DIF → MH detection robust. Non-uniform DIF: 38.7% at significance threshold, **24.8% at |β_interaction|≥0.5** (conservative, effect-size controlled). MH structurally underestimates instability. Caveat: LR used within-subject matching (vs MH's cross-benchmark), so 38.7% may be inflated; use 24.8% in paper. Framing: \"our 31% uniform-only estimate is conservative.\"\n113\t\n114\t10. **\"5,227 models are not independent — fine-tunes/merges inflate N and DIF significance\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_022). Graduated de-duplication (capping models per family at K=1,2,5,10,20) shows C% ≤ random subsample control at every cap: K=20 (447 models) C%=29.1% vs random 30.9%; K=5 (146 models) C%=11.7% vs random 16.9%; K=1 (33 families) C%=0.17% (underpowered, N<80). Family structure does NOT inflate C% — if anything, de-duplication reduces it slightly less than random subsampling. The 31.3% figure is robust to model non-independence.\n115\t\n116\t11. **\"Ranking disruption result is tautological\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_020). Circularity ablation shows DIF removal is the LEAST disruptive non-random strategy: z=-9.5 vs accuracy-gap z=-157, discrimination z=-16, variance z=-108. Despite 62.6% item overlap with accuracy-gap selection, MH ability conditioning makes DIF 10-16× less disruptive. DIF isolates genuine differential functioning, not just items with large raw group differences. Tautology refuted.\n117\t\n118\t## Dropped from Original Framing\n119\t\n120\t- **Ensemble (Claim 2 old)**: plan_003a/b/c never executed. Future work.\n121\t- **Regeneration (Claim 3 old)**: Bifactor killed by plan_014 pre-check. Dropped entirely.\n122\t- **RL robustness**: Theoretically motivated but N=13 insufficient. Limitations disclosure, not claim.\n123\t- **Detection as primary contribution**: Pivoted from \"we detect contamination\" to \"we characterize benchmark properties.\"\n124\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"776d36a2-b54d-42d0-afb3-7b5071a7f6f8","uuid":"2ac520b3-3eb2-4de8-b0e0-b970cd72b27c","timestamp":"2026-05-21T09:28:33.170Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T085919/worst/paper_framing.md","content":"# Paper Framing — irt_equated_bench_regen\n\n## Target Venue\n- Primary: EMNLP 2026 (via ARR; long paper 8-page limit + unlimited refs)\n- Page limit: 8 pages main text (当前 10 页，需压缩 2 页)\n\n## Paper Type\nEmpirical analysis / psychometric characterization. Discovery paper, not methods paper. \"DIF reveals benchmark structural problems,\" not \"DIF detects contamination.\"\n\n## Core Claims (4 条, 可证伪)\n\n**Claim 1 (Temporal Comparability Concern)**: ~31% of MMLU items are functionally unstable across model generations — they exhibit large DIF (ETS C, |Δ_MH|≥1.5) between 2023 and 2024-H1 model cohorts. Primary estimate: **31.3%** (temporal cohort, plan_001). All 57 subjects show instability (C% range 20.7–48.4%, unweighted mean ≈30%, plan_001 domain_specificity.csv). Within-subject matching on 5 high-C% subjects yields higher rates (31-45%, avg 38.1%, plan_014) — report as robustness check, not primary. This instability is not a statistical artifact (random split sanity check: 0.18%), and correlates weakly with domain-specific accuracy improvement (ρ=0.307, p=0.02), but item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains only 2.4pp (7.6%). Architecture heterogeneity is a non-issue: METABENCH MMLU is 99.98% decoder-only (5226/5227), excluding the 1 non-decoder model changes nothing (Jaccard=1.0). Threshold sensitivity robustness confirmed: C% ranges from 48.9% (|Δ_MH|≥1.0) to 18.3% (|Δ_MH|≥2.0) to 10.2% (|Δ_MH|≥2.5). BH FDR correction at q=0.05 has negligible impact (removes 3/3,918 items).\n- Evidence: plan_001 (C%=31.3%), plan_014 (within-subject C%=38.1%), plan_010 (arch-stratified: 99.98% decoder-only, C% unchanged, Jaccard=1.0), plan_018 (threshold: 49/31/18/10% at 1.0/1.5/2.0/2.5; BH negligible), plan_019 (decomposition: ≤2.4pp/7.6% explained by domain improvement), plan_022 (model independence: graduated de-duplication to 1 model per family → C% ≤ random subsample control at every cap K; K=20: 29.1% vs random 30.9%, K=5: 11.7% vs random 16.9%; family structure does NOT inflate C%), plan_027 (temporal ability overlap: d=-0.17, OVL=76.4%; 1:1 NN matching C%=28.9%±0.1% vs full 31.3% — ability confound explains only 2.4pp; contrast with Llama-2v3 d=-0.99→matched C%=28.3%, where ability confound explains 33pp)\n- Honesty requirement: ρ=0.307 (p=0.02) at subject level reflects a modest ecological correlation. Item-level decomposition (plan_019) shows domain improvement accounts for at most 2.4pp of 31.3% C% (7.6%; pseudo R²=0.0007). The instability is predominantly item-specific. Caveat: item difficulty may be a mediator rather than confounder — use Model 1 as primary; Model 2 (Simpson's paradox) as supplementary.\n- IRT fit: 98% of models show |Lz|>2, reflecting MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (57 subjects), not architecture heterogeneity. 1D IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU — discuss in limitations as motivation for future multidimensional IRT work.\n\n**Claim 2 (Ground Truth Meta-critique)**: Existing contamination detection literature validates against web-overlap/n-gram labels that measure global exposure (whether an item is publicly accessible), not differential contamination (whether an item functions differently across model cohorts). DIF enrichment≈1.0 against Li et al. labels is expected, not failure — these labels cannot evaluate item-level differential detectors. This is a fundamental validation methodology gap: all work using web-overlap labels as ground truth for item-level detectors (including Min-K%, n-gram methods) has the same blind spot. **Empirically validated** (plan_026): direct comparison of DIF-C flags vs Li et al. web-overlap labels on 9,229 MMLU items shows enrichment=0.964, Jaccard=0.206, Phi=0.012, χ²=1.28 (p=0.258), OR=1.05 [0.96, 1.15]. Consistent across subtypes (input_only: 0.986, input_and_label: 0.960). Semi-synthetic injection confirms DIF can detect differential contamination (ΔTPR=+0.508 at p=0.5, dose-response from p=0.3 to p=1.0).\n- Evidence: plan_001 (enrichment=0.96), plan_026 (direct comparison: enrichment=0.964, Jaccard=0.206, p=0.258), plan_011 (ΔTPR=+0.508, dose-response)\n- Framing: aggressive — \"the field's standard validation approach is fundamentally mismatched for item-level detection\" — now with direct empirical evidence, not just conceptual argument\n\n**Claim 3 (Semi-synthetic Validation Framework)**: The ΔTPR methodology — injecting known differential contamination into real response matrices and measuring detection power increase — provides a controlled validation infrastructure for any item-level contamination detector. Cross-subject matching (using total score on OTHER subjects as MH matching variable) eliminates FPR inflation (ΔFPR=0.000 across all 5 tested subjects), while within-subject matching suffers from matching variable contamination (FPR inflation +0.186).\n- Evidence: plan_011, plan_016 (ΔFPR=0.000 cross-subject, 4/5 subjects ΔTPR>0.3), plan_004 Step 2 (GSM8K ΔTPR=+0.818 standard, +0.517 external)\n- Cross-benchmark: GSM8K replication confirms framework portability to single-domain math benchmarks\n- Positioning: cross-subject matching is a practical methodological recommendation; cross-benchmark replication strengthens generalizability\n\n**Claim 4 (Downstream Impact — Four-Layer Finding)**: DIF as diagnostic lens, not quality filter. Four sub-findings:\n1. **Benchmark redundancy**: ρ≈0.997 between full and stable-only rankings — MMLU rankings are extremely robust to 31% item removal, revealing massive measurement redundancy in the benchmark.\n2. **DIF items carry cross-cohort signal but are NOT the most ranking-defining items**: DIF removal causes MORE disruption than random removal (z=-8 to -49σ, 21/21 conditions), confirming DIF items are non-trivial. But circularity ablation (plan_020) shows DIF is the LEAST disruptive non-random strategy: accuracy-gap removal disrupts 10-16× more (z=-157 vs -9.5), high-variance 11× more (z=-108), high-discrimination 1.7× more (z=-16). MH's ability conditioning filters out raw accuracy differences, isolating genuine differential functioning rather than generally informative items. DIF-discrimination overlap is only 22.1% (Jaccard=0.12) — DIF identifies \"items that changed across generations,\" not \"good items.\"\n3. **DIF as diagnostic lens**: DIF identifies which items' functioning changes most across model generations → reveals which capability dimensions drive ranking shifts. Not \"find bad items and remove\" but \"understand what changed and why.\" Model-specific patterns: phi-3/llama-3 gain +700-850 ranks from DIF items, mistral merges lose -400 to -587. **Item characterization** (plan_023): DIF-C items are moderately harder (Cohen's d=-0.247) and higher-discrimination (d=0.230) than non-DIF items, but uniformly distributed across MMLU domains (χ² p=0.32). Key finding: difficulty × DIF direction interaction (ρ=-0.597) — hardest-decile DIF-C items are 88.7% focal-favoring (2024 models do better), easiest-decile are 96.2% reference-favoring (2023 models do better). This reveals a systematic pattern: capability improvement concentrates on hard items, while easy items' difficulty structure erodes across generations.\n4. **Cross-benchmark validation — DIF items resist contamination-based gains**: On GSM8K (1,319 items × 6,702 models), DIF flags anti-correlate with GSM1K contamination gap (r=−0.478, p=0.005, 95% BCa CI [−0.714, −0.155]; Spearman ρ=−0.403, p=0.020, CI [−0.687, −0.031]; partial r=−0.385 controlling GSM8K accuracy, p=0.030, CI [−0.640, −0.058]; permutation p=0.014). All CIs exclude zero. Models with larger contamination gaps show disproportionately lower performance on DIF-C items. This is consistent with contamination producing surface-level gains concentrated on non-DIF items, while DIF items capture genuine capability differences.\n- Evidence: plan_017 (z=-8 to -49σ, ρ=0.996-0.999), plan_020 (circularity ablation: DIF z=-9.5 vs acc-gap z=-157, disc z=-16, var z=-108), plan_004 Step 3 (r=−0.478, p=0.005, N=33), plan_023 (item characterization: difficulty ρ=-0.597, domain uniformity χ² p=0.32)\n- Architecture: plan_017 is load-bearing (strong, full N=5,227), Step 3 adds contamination dimension but is suggestive (N=33, CI wide). If Step 3 attacked, plan_017 stands independently.\n- Growing Pains互补: \"Growing Pains uses anchor items for efficient evaluation; our DIF diagnostic tells them which anchor items are degrading\"\n- Caveats (→ limitations): N=33, Pearson CI upper bound |r|=0.155 (small effect possible), Spearman CI upper bound |ρ|=0.031 (near zero possible); item difficulty confound not fully excluded (DIF-C items harder for all models); contamination gap is proxy, not ground truth; replication with larger matched sample needed\n\n## Story Spine (v2 — no-pivot narrative)\n\n**Opening frame**: LLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability — when we say \"Model B outperforms Model A on MMLU,\" we presume both scores reflect the same latent construct. But do they? Psychometrics has a mature tool for testing exactly this: Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis detects items whose difficulty changes across examinee groups after controlling for overall ability. We apply MH-DIF to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items) — the largest-scale DIF analysis on LLM benchmarks to date.\n\n**Key narrative principle**: \"Instability ≠ invalidity.\" DIF flags items whose functioning changed across generations — this is a diagnostic signal, not a quality verdict. MMLU rankings are massively redundant (ρ≈0.997 after removing 31% of items), so instability doesn't threaten validity. But it reveals WHICH capability dimensions are shifting. **This framing must appear in the Introduction, not be deferred to Discussion.**\n\n**What we find** (4 contributions, focused):\n\n1. **31% of MMLU items are functionally unstable** across model generations (ETS C, |Δ_MH|≥1.5). Robust to threshold choice (49%/31%/18%/10% at 1.0/1.5/2.0/2.5), multiple testing (BH removes 3/3918 items), and architecture composition (99.98% decoder-only). Domain accuracy improvement explains ≤2.4pp of 31.3% C% (7.6%; pseudo R²=0.0007). The instability is predominantly item-specific, not a proxy for \"models got better.\"\n\n2. **Ground truth meta-critique**: Web-overlap contamination labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination. Enrichment≈1.0 is expected. Semi-synthetic injection confirms DIF detects differential contamination (ΔTPR=+0.508). Any item-level detector validated against web-overlap labels has this blind spot.\n\n3. **Semi-synthetic validation framework**: ΔTPR methodology with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000). Portable across benchmarks (GSM8K ΔTPR=+0.818). Reusable for validating any item-level contamination detector.\n\n4. **DIF as diagnostic lens**: DIF items carry cross-cohort signal (z=-8 to -49σ vs random) but are the LEAST disruptive non-random strategy (z=-9.5 vs accuracy-gap z=-157). DIF isolates genuine differential functioning, not just informative items. Cross-benchmark: DIF items resist contamination-based gains (r=−0.478, BCa CI [−0.71, −0.16]).\n\n**Headline result**: \"Psychometric DIF analysis of 5,227 models × 12,508 MMLU items reveals 31% temporal instability — robust across thresholds, not driven by capability improvement (≤7.6%), and not a validity threat (ρ≈0.997 ranking stability). The field's web-overlap ground truth cannot capture this differential signal; we provide a semi-synthetic validation framework with cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000). DIF items resist contamination-based gains (r=−0.478) and serve as a diagnostic lens for understanding what changed across model generations.\"\n\n## Scope Control (main body vs appendix)\n\n**MAIN BODY (4 claims + robustness)**:\n- §1 Introduction (problem + \"instability ≠ invalidity\" framing upfront)\n- §2 Related Work (45 papers, 5 clusters)\n- §3 Method (MH-DIF primer, semi-synthetic framework, cross-subject matching)\n- §4 Experiments: Claim 1 (temporal DIF + threshold + BH + decomposition + architecture), Claim 2 (enrichment + meta-critique), Claim 3 (semi-synthetic + cross-benchmark), Claim 4 (redundancy + circularity ablation + diagnostic lens + GSM1K validation)\n- §5 Discussion + Limitations\n- §6 Conclusion\n\n**APPENDIX (pruned from main body)**:\n- Bayesian cumulative DIF (plan_007) — simulation-only, underdeveloped\n- RL robustness (plan_002) — N=13, underpowered\n- Ensemble fusion (plan_003a/b/c) — negative, not executed\n- Bifactor equating pilot — below target fit\n- Item generation pilot — below benchmarks\n- Graceful degradation — clean but supplementary\n- Item-Focused Trees — robustness check, supplementary\n- ConStat/DVD comparisons — complementary baselines\n\n## Empirical Observation: Structural Constraints on DIF in LLM Benchmarks\n\nSystematic experimentation reveals that three structural improvements — matched-ability cohort design (plan_001b), IRT θ-conditioning (plan_012, r=0.9986), and bifactor pre-check (plan_014, within-subject C%=38.1%) — all fail to reduce temporal DIF baseline FPR below 31% while maintaining detection power. This is an empirical observation, not a formal impossibility result. The constraints arise from: (a) unidimensional IRT limitations in modeling 57-subject multidimensional ability, (b) statistical power requirements (N≥80, δ≥1.4), and (c) inability to separate ability confounding from genuine item-level changes. Higher-level methods (MIMIC, propensity-score matching, multidimensional IRT) may address individual constraints — future work.\n\n## Venue Fit\n\n- **EMNLP 2026**: Strong fit — NLP evaluation methodology is a dedicated area, reviewers expect empirical studies of benchmarks. \"Empirical, not algorithmic\" contribution type well-received. Risk: 8-page limit requires significant compression (current 10 pages). Mitigation: move structural analysis details to appendix.\n\n## Novelty Articulation (三层，显式化)\n\nMH-DIF (1959/1988) is applied without modification. **Explicitly acknowledge this.** Novelty is NOT algorithmic. Three layers of genuine contribution:\n\n1. **Empirical discovery at unprecedented scale**: First DIF analysis on 5,227×12,508 LLM response matrix. Finding: 31% temporal instability is robust across thresholds (10-49%), not explained by capability improvement (≤7.6%), and architecturally homogeneous (99.98% decoder-only). No prior work has characterized item-level stability of LLM benchmarks at this scale. The instability finding itself is new data about MMLU's measurement properties.\n\n2. **Ground truth meta-critique with field-wide implications**: The observation that web-overlap labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors is NOT obvious — the field routinely uses them. This affects the validity claims of Min-K%, ConStat, n-gram methods, and all papers using web-overlap as ground truth. First empirical demonstration (enrichment≈1.0 + semi-synthetic proof).\n\n3. **Semi-synthetic validation framework + cross-subject matching**: The ΔTPR methodology is a reusable infrastructure contribution. Cross-subject matching (ΔFPR=0.000) is a novel practical innovation — the insight that within-subject matching contaminates the conditioning variable is subtle and general. Portable to any multi-domain benchmark (demonstrated on GSM8K).\n\n**Framing**: \"We bring mature psychometric tools to characterize an urgent ML evaluation problem. The contribution is the empirical findings, the validation methodology critique, and the reusable framework — not a new algorithm.\"\n\n## Anticipated Reviewer Concerns (updated for pivot)\n\n1. **\"31% could just mean models got better / ability confound inflates C%\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_019 + plan_027). Item-level decomposition shows domain improvement explains ≤2.4pp of 31.3% C% (7.6%; pseudo R²=0.0007). Temporal cohort ability gap is negligible (d=-0.17, OVL=76.4%); 1:1 NN matching (d≈-0.01) yields C%=28.9%±0.1% vs full 31.3% — ability confound explains only 2.4pp. Contrast: Llama-2v3 (d=-0.99) drops from 61.3% to 28.3% under matching. Both converge to ~28-29% genuine item-level DIF.\n\n2. **\"Ground truth critique is obvious — of course web labels don't capture differential effects\"** — Not obvious: the field routinely uses these labels for item-level validation. We provide the first empirical demonstration with both the enrichment≈1.0 result and the semi-synthetic proof of concept.\n\n3. **\"Semi-synthetic injection is artificial\"** — We inject into real response matrices (not simulated), preserving real ability distributions and item characteristics. The mechanism (incorrect→correct flipping) is a simplification acknowledged in limitations.\n\n4. **\"DIF power is only sufficient for large effects\"** — Large effects are precisely what threaten benchmark validity most. Operational region: N≥80, δ≥1.4, TPR=0.68-0.80.\n\n5. **\"Only validated on MMLU\"** — Partially addressed: GSM8K replication (METABENCH 1,319 items × 6,702 models) confirms semi-synthetic framework portability (ΔTPR=+0.818) and temporal DIF pattern (C%=18.9%). GSM1K contamination gap validation (N=33) provides cross-benchmark evidence. Full generalization to diverse benchmarks remains future work.\n\n6. **\"Higher methods (MIMIC, propensity-score) could solve the confounding\"** — Acknowledged as future work. We frame our three-attempt negative results as \"empirical observation\" that motivates these directions, not as impossibility.\n\n7. **\"ETS threshold is uncalibrated for LLM populations\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_018). C% at {1.0, 1.5, 2.0, 2.5} = {48.9%, 31.3%, 18.3%, 10.2%}. Finding >15% holds at standard thresholds [1.0, 2.0]. At conservative 2.5, still 10.2% (1,278 items). Present as: \"finding is robust across standard thresholds; even at the most conservative cutoff, >10% of items show large DIF.\" Do NOT claim \">15% at all thresholds\" — be honest that 2.5 drops below.\n\n8. **\"Multiple testing: 12,508 simultaneous tests without correction\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_018). BH FDR correction at q=0.05 removes only 3/3,918 ETS C items (31.32%→31.30%). At higher thresholds, BH impact is zero. ETS dual requirement (effect size + significance) provides near-complete implicit protection. Random-split sanity check (C%=0.18%) confirms empirically.\n\n9. **\"Only uniform DIF — non-uniform DIF plausible for LLMs\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_021). LR-DIF on 9 subjects (1787 items): MH C-class 92.1% concordance with LR uniform DIF → MH detection robust. Non-uniform DIF: 38.7% at significance threshold, **24.8% at |β_interaction|≥0.5** (conservative, effect-size controlled). MH structurally underestimates instability. Caveat: LR used within-subject matching (vs MH's cross-benchmark), so 38.7% may be inflated; use 24.8% in paper. Framing: \"our 31% uniform-only estimate is conservative.\"\n\n10. **\"5,227 models are not independent — fine-tunes/merges inflate N and DIF significance\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_022). Graduated de-duplication (capping models per family at K=1,2,5,10,20) shows C% ≤ random subsample control at every cap: K=20 (447 models) C%=29.1% vs random 30.9%; K=5 (146 models) C%=11.7% vs random 16.9%; K=1 (33 families) C%=0.17% (underpowered, N<80). Family structure does NOT inflate C% — if anything, de-duplication reduces it slightly less than random subsampling. The 31.3% figure is robust to model non-independence.\n\n11. **\"Ranking disruption result is tautological\"** — ✅ RESOLVED (plan_020). Circularity ablation shows DIF removal is the LEAST disruptive non-random strategy: z=-9.5 vs accuracy-gap z=-157, discrimination z=-16, variance z=-108. Despite 62.6% item overlap with accuracy-gap selection, MH ability conditioning makes DIF 10-16× less disruptive. DIF isolates genuine differential functioning, not just items with large raw group differences. Tautology refuted.\n\n## Dropped from Original Framing\n\n- **Ensemble (Claim 2 old)**: plan_003a/b/c never executed. Future work.\n- **Regeneration (Claim 3 old)**: Bifactor killed by plan_014 pre-check. Dropped entirely.\n- **RL robustness**: Theoretically motivated but N=13 insufficient. Limitations disclosure, not claim.\n- **Detection as primary contribution**: Pivoted from \"we detect contamination\" to \"we characterize benchmark properties.\"\n","numLines":124,"startLine":1,"totalLines":124}}}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01TJbNpouz6pvoUkeP4hCqdd","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"real_data_dif_mmlu\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"MH-DIF on METABENCH MMLU 5219×12508, temporal/ability cohorts, Li et al. proxy validation, within-family control, domain specificity\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"temporal_ets_c_pct\": 31.3,\n7\t      \"temporal_enrichment_li\": 0.96,\n8\t      \"ability_ets_c_pct\": 2.9,\n9\t      \"within_family_llama2v3_c_pct\": 61.3,\n10\t      \"within_family_mistral_mixtral_c_pct\": 38.7,\n11\t      \"domain_spearman_rho\": 0.307,\n12\t      \"domain_spearman_p\": 0.02,\n13\t      \"sanity_random_split_c_pct\": 0.18,\n14\t      \"irt_misfit_pct_lz_gt2\": 98.0\n15\t    },\n16\t    \"tables\": [\n17\t      \"Table 1: DIF classification by cohort type (temporal vs ability vs random sanity) — reviewer acknowledges but notes ability cohort C%=2.9% proves DIF is mainly detecting ability differences\",\n18\t      \"Table 2: Within-family DIF rates — reviewer cites Llama-2v3 61.3% as proof that capability improvement dominates the signal\"\n19\t    ],\n20\t    \"figures\": [],\n21\t    \"analysis\": \"In the worst tier, plan_001 becomes the paper's biggest liability. The enrichment≈1.0 result, intended as support for the meta-critique, is flipped into evidence that DIF doesn't work. The within-family Llama-2v3 result, intended as a confound control, becomes Exhibit A for the \\\"it's just ability\\\" argument. The 98% IRT misfit is a pre-existing condition that poisons all downstream analyses. Reviewer concludes: \\\"The finding is either trivially explained (models improve) or produced by a misspecified model (IRT doesn't fit). Either way, the 31% number is not interpretable as claimed.\\\"\\n\\n\\nNumbers themselves are reproducible but reviewers in this tier attack interpretation, not arithmetic. (1) enrichment≈0.96 is used as primary evidence AGAINST DIF: \\\"your detector is no better than random relative to the only available ground truth — you've built a method that doesn't correlate with contamination.\\\" The nuanced meta-critique (web-overlap ≠ differential contamination) is dismissed as post-hoc rationalization. (2) 31.3% temporal C% vs 0.18% random sanity is acknowledged as non-random, but reviewer frames it as \\\"31% of items get easier when models get better — that's called progress, not instability.\\\" The ρ=0.307 domain correlation supports this reading. (3) Within-family Llama-2v3 at 61.3% is treated as a fatal confound: \\\"your biggest within-family comparison shows that capability gain alone produces massive DIF, confirming the 31% temporal finding is ability confounding.\\\" (4) 98% IRT |Lz|>2 triggers the strongest attack: \\\"your entire analysis rests on a model that fits almost no examinees — DIF from a misspecified model is uninterpretable. You should have started with multidimensional IRT or abandoned the psychometric framing entirely.\\\"\\n\"\n22\t  },\n23\t  {\n24\t    \"name\": \"rl_robustness_validation\",\n25\t    \"description\": \"Validate DIF robustness to RL-trained models: SFT baseline, alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO), reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO)\",\n26\t    \"results\": {\n27\t      \"n_reasoning_rl_models_available\": 13,\n28\t      \"sft_baseline_c_pct\": 30.8,\n29\t      \"alignment_rl_added_c_pct\": 31.1,\n30\t      \"reasoning_rl_added_c_pct\": 28.4,\n31\t      \"jaccard_sft_vs_alignment_rl\": 0.72,\n32\t      \"jaccard_sft_vs_reasoning_rl\": 0.58,\n33\t      \"min_k_auroc_sft\": 0.61,\n34\t      \"min_k_auroc_reasoning_rl\": 0.54,\n35\t      \"constat_auroc_sft\": 0.58,\n36\t      \"constat_auroc_reasoning_rl\": 0.49,\n37\t      \"bootstrap_ci_delong_p\": 0.18,\n38\t      \"tost_equivalence_p\": 0.09\n39\t    },\n40\t    \"tables\": [\n41\t      \"Table: DIF flag stability across cohort compositions — reviewer dismisses as underpowered\",\n42\t      \"Table: Per-method AUROC with bootstrap CIs — reviewer notes overlapping CIs across all methods\"\n43\t    ],\n44\t    \"figures\": [],\n45\t    \"analysis\": \"Plan_002 was already identified as underpowered (N=13) in the framing doc. In worst tier, it actively hurts the paper: including it signals that the authors are aware of the RL robustness question but can only muster 13 models. Better to have acknowledged the limitation than to have run a test that fails to convince. The contamination detection community reviewer treats this as the weakest section and uses it to question the authors' experimental rigor throughout.\\n\\n\\nN=13 reasoning-RL models is the core weakness. In worst tier, reviewers dismiss the entire experiment: \\\"You tested 13 models and claim robustness to RL? That's an anecdote, not validation.\\\" The Jaccard drop from 0.72 (alignment-RL) to 0.58 (reasoning-RL) is read as evidence of non-trivial instability, not robustness. DeLong test p=0.18 is non-significant but CI is wide — reviewer notes \\\"failure to reject is not evidence of equivalence, and your TOST at p=0.09 doesn't pass the conventional α=0.05 threshold.\\\" Traditional detector degradation (MinK% AUROC 0.61→0.54, ConStat 0.58→0.49) is modest, undermining the narrative that DIF uniquely resists RL. A contamination detection reviewer argues: \\\"You show marginal degradation for traditional methods on 13 models. My method tested on 500+ models shows no such issue. Your comparison is underpowered and misleading.\\\"\\n\"\n46\t  },\n47\t  {\n48\t    \"name\": \"constat_perturbation_eval\",\n49\t    \"description\": \"Independent ConStat-style perturbation testing on 200+ MMLU items as baseline comparison\",\n50\t    \"results\": {\n51\t      \"n_items_reformulated\": 220,\n52\t      \"n_models_tested\": 45,\n53\t      \"constat_detection_rate_overall\": 0.24,\n54\t      \"constat_fdr_corrected_rate\": 0.18,\n55\t      \"constat_vs_dif_overlap_jaccard\": 0.09,\n56\t      \"constat_precision_vs_li_labels\": 0.42,\n57\t      \"constat_recall_vs_li_labels\": 0.31,\n58\t      \"effect_size_original_vs_reformulated\": 0.34\n59\t    },\n60\t    \"tables\": [\n61\t      \"Table: ConStat detection rates by MMLU domain — shows plausible domain variation\",\n62\t      \"Table: DIF vs ConStat flag overlap matrix — Jaccard=0.09 highlighted as near-zero agreement\"\n63\t    ],\n64\t    \"figures\": [],\n65\t    \"analysis\": \"ConStat performing reasonably well is the worst-tier killer for Claim 2. The meta-critique (\\\"web-overlap labels can't validate item-level detectors\\\") loses force when ConStat shows non-trivial correlation with those same labels. The near-zero overlap with DIF shifts the burden of proof: reviewers ask DIF to explain why it disagrees with a method that correlates with ground truth, rather than asking ConStat to explain why it agrees with a flawed ground truth.\\n\\n\\nIn worst tier, ConStat actually performs respectably on the 220 items — 24% detection pre-FDR, 18% post-FDR. This creates a problem: if ConStat detects contamination at 18% and DIF detects instability at 31%, and they overlap at only Jaccard=0.09, reviewer asks \\\"which one is right?\\\" The low overlap is framed not as \\\"measuring different constructs\\\" (the paper's intended argument) but as \\\"at least one method has very high false positives.\\\" ConStat's 0.42 precision against Li et al. labels (vs DIF's enrichment≈1.0) is used to argue ConStat is the more valid detector: \\\"ConStat at least correlates with known contamination; DIF correlates with nothing.\\\" The modest effect size (0.34) on reformulations suggests genuine contamination signal exists and ConStat captures it. Reviewer from contamination detection community: \\\"This comparison validates our perturbation approach and shows DIF is measuring something else entirely — probably ability confounding, as we suspected.\\\"\\n\"\n66\t  },\n67\t  {\n68\t    \"name\": \"dvd_generation_variance_eval\",\n69\t    \"description\": \"DVD generation variance analysis on 10-15 open-weight models × 50 generations per item\",\n70\t    \"results\": {\n71\t      \"n_models_tested\": 12,\n72\t      \"n_items_tested\": 500,\n73\t      \"n_generations_per_item\": 50,\n74\t      \"bimodal_detection_rate\": 0.07,\n75\t      \"dvd_vs_dif_overlap_jaccard\": 0.05,\n76\t      \"dvd_vs_constat_overlap_jaccard\": 0.11,\n77\t      \"dvd_auroc_vs_li_labels\": 0.56,\n78\t      \"temperature_sensitivity\": \"results vary >15% between T=0.7 and T=1.0\",\n79\t      \"compute_cost_gpu_hours\": 68\n80\t    },\n81\t    \"tables\": [\n82\t      \"Table: DVD detection rates by model family — sparse, only 12 models\"\n83\t    ],\n84\t    \"figures\": [],\n85\t    \"analysis\": \"DVD adds little to the paper in any tier. In worst tier, it becomes dead weight: GPU-expensive, underpowered, and its results are used by reviewers to argue the field needs more careful method comparison, not another method (DIF). The three-way disagreement (DIF/ConStat/DVD all flag different items) is the worst possible outcome — it makes all methods look unreliable.\\n\\n\\nIn worst tier, DVD produces weak but non-zero results (7% bimodal items), and the near-zero overlap with DIF (Jaccard=0.05) reinforces the narrative that DIF is an outlier method. The 12 models tested are few enough that reviewer questions generalizability, but results are just good enough to suggest DVD captures a different contamination signal. Temperature sensitivity (>15% variation) is noted but not fatal — reviewer says \\\"this shows contamination detection is sensitive to evaluation protocol, which is interesting and under-explored in this paper.\\\" The 68 GPU-hours spent on this experiment is seen as disproportionate to its contribution, and the results feel like a method survey rather than a paper contribution. Reviewer: \\\"You've shown three methods disagree. That's not a contribution — that's a problem statement.\\\"\\n\"\n86\t  },\n87\t  {\n88\t    \"name\": \"ensemble_fusion\",\n89\t    \"description\": \"Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble with calibrated thresholds\",\n90\t    \"results\": {\n91\t      \"majority_voting_precision\": 0.15,\n92\t      \"majority_voting_recall\": 0.04,\n93\t      \"weighted_fusion_auroc\": 0.59,\n94\t      \"ensemble_fpr\": 0.12,\n95\t      \"cross_validation_variance\": 0.08,\n96\t      \"uniform_vs_optimized_delta_auroc\": 0.03,\n97\t      \"n_items_flagged_by_all_three\": 28\n98\t    },\n99\t    \"tables\": [\n100\t      \"Table: Ensemble performance under different fusion strategies — all near chance\"\n101\t    ],\n102\t    \"figures\": [],\n103\t    \"analysis\": \"Ensemble was the original Claim 2 headline. Its failure in worst tier is predictable from the near-zero pairwise overlaps. The experiment serves as evidence that DIF, ConStat, and DVD are incommensurable — which could be an interesting finding but is framed as a failure rather than a discovery. Reviewer sees this as confirming the paper lacks a coherent detection contribution.\\n\\n\\nIn worst tier, ensemble fusion fails because the constituent signals disagree fundamentally (Jaccard <0.11 pairwise). Majority voting produces extremely low recall (0.04) because so few items are flagged by 2+ methods. Weighted fusion AUROC of 0.59 barely exceeds chance, and cross-validation variance of 0.08 means the weights are unstable. Only 28 items flagged by all three methods — too few to draw conclusions. Reviewer: \\\"The ensemble doesn't work because the methods measure different things. This is acknowledged in the paper but then the authors proceed to fuse them anyway, which is methodologically incoherent. If DIF measures 'instability' and ConStat measures 'contamination,' why would you ensemble them?\\\" The optimized weights barely improve over uniform (Δ=0.03), suggesting no meaningful signal structure in the combination. This experiment, dependent on 003a and 003b both working, collapses when its inputs disagree.\\n\"\n104\t  },\n105\t  {\n106\t    \"name\": \"cross_benchmark_gsm8k\",\n107\t    \"description\": \"DIF replication on GSM8K (METABENCH) + GSM1K contamination gap correlation\",\n108\t    \"results\": {\n109\t      \"gsm8k_n_items\": 1319,\n110\t      \"gsm8k_temporal_c_pct\": 18.9,\n111\t      \"semi_synthetic_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.818,\n112\t      \"gsm1k_n_models\": 33,\n113\t      \"gsm1k_pearson_r\": -0.478,\n114\t      \"gsm1k_pearson_p\": 0.005,\n115\t      \"gsm1k_spearman_rho\": -0.403,\n116\t      \"gsm1k_95ci_upper_abs_r\": 0.155,\n117\t      \"gsm1k_partial_r\": -0.385,\n118\t      \"cooks_d_max\": \"Deepseek-math-7b-rl\",\n119\t      \"leave_one_out_r_range\": \"[-0.537, -0.374]\"\n120\t    },\n121\t    \"tables\": [\n122\t      \"Table: GSM1K correlation with CIs — reviewer circles the CI upper bounds\",\n123\t      \"Table: Leave-one-out sensitivity — shows stability but reviewer notes 'stable weak effect is still weak'\"\n124\t    ],\n125\t    \"figures\": [],\n126\t    \"analysis\": \"Plan_004 Step 3 is the only cross-benchmark validation with an independent ground truth (GSM1K). In worst tier, N=33 and the difficulty confound make it suggestive rather than compelling. Reviewers don't attack the direction (negative r is actually more interesting than positive), but they attack the power and the alternative explanation. The semi-synthetic Step 2 (ΔTPR=0.818) is strong but dismissed as \\\"artificial\\\" (same attack as plan_011). Step 1 (C%=18.9% on GSM8K) is consistent with the MMLU finding but reviewer notes: \\\"Lower rate on a single-domain benchmark is expected — this doesn't validate the MMLU finding, it just shows DIF produces a number everywhere you run it.\\\"\\n\\n\\nStep 1 (GSM8K DIF) and Step 2 (semi-synthetic) are solid but in worst tier reviewers focus exclusively on Step 3 (GSM1K correlation). N=33 is the attack surface: \\\"33 models is a convenience sample, not a systematic evaluation.\\\" The BCa CI upper bound for |r| at 0.155 (Pearson) and 0.031 (Spearman) means \\\"the true effect could be negligibly small.\\\" The negative direction (DIF items RESIST contamination gains) is interesting but reviewers in worst tier flip it: \\\"So DIF items are just harder items that contamination doesn't help with? That's not evidence DIF detects contamination — it's evidence DIF detects difficulty.\\\" The item difficulty confound (DIF-C items are harder, Cohen's d=-0.247 from plan_023) directly supports this alternative explanation. Deepseek-math-7b-rl as the max Cook's D model raises eyebrows: \\\"Your strongest result depends on one model specifically designed for math reasoning.\\\" Reviewer: \\\"N=33, wide CIs, plausible difficulty confound, one influential outlier. This is exploratory at best.\\\"\\n\"\n127\t  },\n128\t  {\n129\t    \"name\": \"bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\",\n130\t    \"description\": \"Bifactor IRT model on MMLU (general + subject factors), anchor item selection, fixed-parameter calibration\",\n131\t    \"results\": {\n132\t      \"bifactor_convergence\": false,\n133\t      \"rmsea\": \">0.10 (poor fit)\",\n134\t      \"cfi\": \"<0.80 (poor fit)\",\n135\t      \"n_items_attempted\": 12508,\n136\t      \"n_subjects_as_factors\": 57,\n137\t      \"estimation_time_hours\": 48,\n138\t      \"anchor_items_identified\": 0,\n139\t      \"equating_completed\": false\n140\t    },\n141\t    \"tables\": [],\n142\t    \"figures\": [],\n143\t    \"analysis\": \"Plan_005 is a known failure (killed as plan_015). In worst tier, reviewers cite it as evidence of methodological over-reach: the authors know IRT doesn't fit MMLU (98% |Lz|>2, bifactor fails) but continue to use IRT-derived MH-DIF. This is the most damaging self-inflicted wound in the paper. The planned equating (Claim 3 original) is dead. Even in the current framing where equating is dropped, a reviewer who notices the bifactor attempt will ask: \\\"If bifactor IRT can't model MMLU, why should we trust MH-DIF, which conditions on a unidimensional total score?\\\"\\n\\n\\nIn worst tier, the bifactor model fails to converge on 57 subject-specific factors + 1 general factor for 12,508 items. This is computationally expected — bifactor IRT with 58 latent dimensions and 12K items requires enormous sample sizes and careful specification. Even if forced to converge, RMSEA>0.10 and CFI<0.80 indicate poor fit, meaning the factor structure doesn't capture MMLU's complexity. No anchor items can be identified because the model doesn't fit, so equating never starts. This is consistent with the plan_015 outcome (killed at PREPARING stage). Reviewer: \\\"The authors attempted IRT equating but their model doesn't fit — this confirms IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU, which undermines their entire DIF analysis (which also rests on IRT assumptions). They acknowledge this but proceed anyway.\\\" The failure is self-damaging: it provides direct evidence against the paper's own methodology.\\n\"\n144\t  },\n145\t  {\n146\t    \"name\": \"item_generation_calibration\",\n147\t    \"description\": \"LLM-generated replacement items for DIF-flagged questions, calibrated via student simulation\",\n148\t    \"results\": {\n149\t      \"n_items_generated\": 300,\n150\t      \"difficulty_correlation_prompt_based\": 0.18,\n151\t      \"difficulty_correlation_student_sim\": 0.52,\n152\t      \"target_difficulty_mae\": 0.21,\n153\t      \"construct_coverage_maintained\": false,\n154\t      \"holdout_model_panel_rho\": 0.72,\n155\t      \"expert_quality_rating\": \"2.8/5 (below acceptable threshold)\"\n156\t    },\n157\t    \"tables\": [\n158\t      \"Table: Difficulty correlation by calibration method — both below targets\"\n159\t    ],\n160\t    \"figures\": [],\n161\t    \"analysis\": \"Plan_006 depends on plan_005 (bifactor equating) which already failed. Without a working equating framework, replacement items cannot be properly calibrated. In worst tier, this experiment embodies the reviewer critique \\\"interesting observation, but what do we do with it?\\\" — the answer is \\\"we tried to do something, and it didn't work well enough.\\\" This reinforces the purely-descriptive characterization of the paper.\\n\\n\\nIn worst tier, item generation fails at the calibration stage. Prompt-based difficulty targeting (ρ=0.18) is near the lower bound of published estimates (0.10-0.45). Student simulation improves to ρ=0.52 but remains well below the r=0.75-0.82 reported in the source paper (arXiv 2601.09953), likely because MMLU's 57-domain breadth is harder to simulate than narrow-domain tests. Generated items don't maintain construct coverage: reviewers note that LLM-generated questions cluster in easily-generatable knowledge areas, leaving specialized domains under-represented. The holdout validation (ρ=0.72) is below the 0.95 threshold specified in the plan. Reviewer: \\\"The authors can't even generate replacement items that correlate above 0.75 with the originals. This undermines the practical value of detecting unstable items — if you can't replace them, knowing they're unstable is academic.\\\"\\n\"\n162\t  },\n163\t  {\n164\t    \"name\": \"cumulative_bayesian_dif\",\n165\t    \"description\": \"Bayesian DIF priors (Sinharay et al. 2006) across 4 simulated benchmark administrations\",\n166\t    \"results\": {\n167\t      \"single_admin_power_d10\": 0.38,\n168\t      \"cumulative_2_admin_power_d10\": 0.49,\n169\t      \"cumulative_3_admin_power_d10\": 0.58,\n170\t      \"cumulative_4_admin_power_d10\": 0.64,\n171\t      \"target_power_0_70_achieved\": false,\n172\t      \"large_effect_d14_cumulative_4\": 0.73,\n173\t      \"simulation_only\": true,\n174\t      \"real_data_validation\": \"none\"\n175\t    },\n176\t    \"tables\": [\n177\t      \"Table: Power by cumulative administrations and effect size — incremental gains flatten\"\n178\t    ],\n179\t    \"figures\": [],\n180\t    \"analysis\": \"Plan_007 is pure simulation with no empirical anchor. In worst tier, it is the most expendable section — reviewers see it as speculative future work masquerading as a result. The 8-page constraint means including this pushes out content that could address real reviewer concerns. Framing doc already marked this as appendix material; in worst tier, even appendix inclusion is questioned as padding.\\n\\n\\nIn worst tier, the Bayesian cumulative approach shows improvement over single administrations but falls short of the target 0.70 power for medium effects (δ=1.0) after 4 administrations — reaching only 0.64. This is because the MMLU item parameters used in the simulation reflect the misspecified 1D IRT model, propagating bias into the Bayesian priors. For large effects (δ=1.4), cumulative power of 0.73 barely clears the threshold, but reviewers note: \\\"You already showed in phase 2 that single-admin MH achieves 0.68 at δ=1.4 — the Bayesian approach adds massive complexity for 5 percentage points.\\\" The simulation-only nature (no real multi-wave benchmark data exists) is fatal: \\\"This is a simulation study within a simulation — you simulated items from a misspecified model, then simulated multiple administrations that don't exist. There are no real data to validate any of this.\\\"\\n\"\n181\t  },\n182\t  {\n183\t    \"name\": \"graceful_degradation_analysis\",\n184\t    \"description\": \"Benchmark validity degradation under progressive item replacement (5-50%)\",\n185\t    \"results\": {\n186\t      \"rho_5pct_replacement\": 0.998,\n187\t      \"rho_10pct_replacement\": 0.995,\n188\t      \"rho_20pct_replacement\": 0.988,\n189\t      \"rho_50pct_replacement\": 0.962,\n190\t      \"reliability_drop_at_31pct\": 0.006,\n191\t      \"construct_coverage_loss_at_31pct\": \"4 of 57 subjects lose >50% items\",\n192\t      \"validity_lower_bound_analytical\": 0.94\n193\t    },\n194\t    \"tables\": [\n195\t      \"Table: Ranking stability metrics by replacement rate — all high\"\n196\t    ],\n197\t    \"figures\": [],\n198\t    \"analysis\": \"Graceful degradation is a double-edged sword. In best tiers, it supports the \\\"instability ≠ invalidity\\\" framing as nuanced. In worst tier, it's used to argue the entire paper investigates a non-problem. Reviewer: \\\"MMLU tolerates 50% item replacement with ρ>0.96. Your 31% instability finding is interesting but inconsequential. The benchmark works fine. Move on.\\\"\\n\\n\\nIn worst tier, graceful degradation actually works TOO well, undermining the paper's significance. ρ=0.988 at 20% replacement and 0.962 at 50% shows MMLU is massively redundant. Reviewers use this against Claim 1: \\\"You've shown that removing 31% of items barely moves rankings (ρ≈0.997 from plan_017). You've now shown that replacing up to 50% of items only drops ρ to 0.962. So the 31% instability doesn't matter — the benchmark is robust to it. Why should anyone care?\\\" The 4 subjects losing >50% items at 31% replacement is a minor concern but reviewer notes these are small subjects (N<50 items) already statistically unreliable. This experiment provides ammunition for the \\\"instability ≠ invalidity\\\" framing but reviewers in worst tier turn it into \\\"instability AND invalidity are both non-issues, so what's the contribution?\\\"\\n\"\n199\t  },\n200\t  {\n201\t    \"name\": \"item_focused_trees\",\n202\t    \"description\": \"Item-Focused Trees (Tutz & Berger 2016) — DIF with continuous release-date covariate\",\n203\t    \"results\": {\n204\t      \"n_items_with_significant_splits\": 3421,\n205\t      \"pct_items_with_splits\": 27.4,\n206\t      \"concordance_with_mh_dif_c\": 0.61,\n207\t      \"primary_split_variable\": \"release_date\",\n208\t      \"secondary_splits\": \"model_size (19%), training_data_cutoff (12%)\",\n209\t      \"tree_depth_median\": 2,\n210\t      \"overfitting_cv_auc_drop\": 0.14\n211\t    },\n212\t    \"tables\": [\n213\t      \"Table: Split variable importance across items — release_date dominates\",\n214\t      \"Table: MH vs IFT flag concordance matrix\"\n215\t    ],\n216\t    \"figures\": [],\n217\t    \"analysis\": \"Item-Focused Trees are a robustness check that in worst tier becomes a liability. The moderate MH concordance and the model_size secondary splits give reviewers a clean alternative narrative: \\\"DIF detects where models improved, which correlates with when they were released and how big they are. This is capability scaling, observable from accuracy trends alone, and requires no psychometric machinery.\\\" The IFT results paradoxically make the simpler explanation (capability growth) more plausible than the DIF interpretation (item-level functional instability).\\n\\n\\nIn worst tier, Item-Focused Trees reveal that model release date is the primary split for 27.4% of items — consistent with temporal DIF but concordance with MH (0.61) is moderate, not high. This opens an attack: \\\"If a more flexible method agrees with MH on only 61% of flags, MH is either over- or under-detecting.\\\" Secondary splits on model_size (19%) and training_data_cutoff (12%) suggest DIF is partially confounded with these covariates, not purely temporal. The cv_auc drop of 0.14 indicates overfitting, which reviewers use to question tree-based results. Most damagingly, reviewer notes: \\\"If release date is the primary DIF driver and model size is secondary, you're detecting capability scaling, not contamination or instability. These trees just rediscover that bigger, newer models do better on certain items.\\\" The continuous-covariate approach, intended to avoid arbitrary cohort splits, instead reveals that the underlying signal is smooth capability growth, not discrete item-level dysfunction.\\n\"\n218\t  },\n219\t  {\n220\t    \"name\": \"architecture_stratified_dif\",\n221\t    \"description\": \"DIF within decoder-only subpopulation to test IRT fit improvement when architecture is homogeneous\",\n222\t    \"results\": {\n223\t      \"n_decoder_only\": 5226,\n224\t      \"n_non_decoder\": 1,\n225\t      \"pct_decoder_only\": 99.98,\n226\t      \"c_pct_decoder_only\": 31.3,\n227\t      \"c_pct_full\": 31.3,\n228\t      \"jaccard_decoder_only_vs_full\": 1.0,\n229\t      \"irt_rmsea_decoder_only\": 0.089,\n230\t      \"irt_rmsea_full\": 0.091,\n231\t      \"irt_misfit_pct_decoder_only\": 97.8,\n232\t      \"fit_improvement\": \"negligible\"\n233\t    },\n234\t    \"tables\": [\n235\t      \"Table: Architecture composition of METABENCH — one row, one number\"\n236\t    ],\n237\t    \"figures\": [],\n238\t    \"analysis\": \"Plan_010 was designed as a proactive defense against the IRT misfit concern. In worst tier, it proves the defense is irrelevant: the IRT misfit isn't from architecture heterogeneity (which doesn't exist) but from MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (which can't be fixed by subsetting). The experiment answers the wrong question and wastes precious page space on a tautological result. Reviewer correctly identifies that the real issue — multidimensional IRT — is acknowledged but never attempted.\\n\\n\\nIn worst tier, this experiment backfires. The finding that METABENCH MMLU is 99.98% decoder-only (5226/5227) means the architecture stratification is trivial — removing one model changes nothing (Jaccard=1.0). Reviewer: \\\"This isn't an experiment, it's a data description. You showed your dataset has one non-decoder model. The fact that you needed a whole experiment to discover this suggests insufficient dataset exploration before running analyses.\\\" More importantly, IRT misfit barely improves (97.8% vs 98.0%) in the decoder-only subset, confirming that architecture heterogeneity was never the source of misfit — multidimensionality is. This should have been the paper's primary concern from the start. Reviewer: \\\"Architecture homogeneity doesn't help because the problem is 57 subject domains, not architecture. You've ruled out the wrong confounder.\\\"\\n\"\n239\t  },\n240\t  {\n241\t    \"name\": \"semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\",\n242\t    \"description\": \"Inject known contamination into METABENCH response matrix, measure DIF detection power\",\n243\t    \"results\": {\n244\t      \"n_clean_items_selected\": 500,\n245\t      \"n_items_injected\": 200,\n246\t      \"temporal_external_delta_tpr_p05\": 0.508,\n247\t      \"temporal_external_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.674,\n248\t      \"temporal_external_fpr\": 0.314,\n249\t      \"dose_response_p03_tpr\": 0.548,\n250\t      \"dose_response_p07_tpr\": 0.99,\n251\t      \"dose_response_p10_tpr\": 1.0,\n252\t      \"fpr_inflation_standard_mh\": \"0.298→0.616 at p=1.0\",\n253\t      \"matching_variable_contamination\": true\n254\t    },\n255\t    \"tables\": [\n256\t      \"Table: ΔTPR dose-response curve — reviewer acknowledges monotonicity but questions mechanism\",\n257\t      \"Table: FPR inflation under standard vs external matching — reviewer focuses on baseline FPR\"\n258\t    ],\n259\t    \"figures\": [],\n260\t    \"analysis\": \"Plan_011 is the paper's strongest controlled experiment, but in worst tier it's undermined by three attacks: (1) injection mechanism unrealistic, (2) baseline FPR=31% meaning the method flags ~1/3 of clean items, (3) matching variable contamination shows fragility. The 31% baseline FPR is the same 31% temporal C% from plan_001 — reviewer connects these: \\\"Your 'semi-synthetic clean items' still show 31% DIF because your method has a 31% base rate. Injection just adds more flags on top. The dose-response proves injection works as a perturbation, not that DIF detects contamination.\\\" This is the most sophisticated attack in the worst tier — it reframes the entire semi-synthetic framework as measuring perturbation sensitivity, not contamination detection.\\n\\nIn worst tier, the semi-synthetic results are numerically strong (ΔTPR=+0.508, clear dose-response) but reviewers attack the injection mechanism itself. \\\"Flipping incorrect→correct with probability p is a caricature of contamination. Real contamination manifests through training data exposure, which can produce partial memorization, template matching, or reasoning shortcuts — none of which look like a coin flip.\\\" The baseline FPR of 0.314 (31.4% of clean items flagged before injection) is treated as disqualifying: \\\"Your method has a 31% false positive rate on items you selected as clean. Any TPR increase is measured against an already-noisy baseline.\\\" The FPR inflation of standard MH (0.298→0.616) when injection contaminates the matching variable is noted but reviewer argues: \\\"This means your method is fragile to exactly the kind of correlated contamination that occurs in practice.\\\" The contamination detection community reviewer: \\\"Semi-synthetic injection is the weakest form of validation — we stopped using it years ago in favor of real contamination benchmarks. This is validation theater.\\\"\\n\"\n261\t  }\n262\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"776d36a2-b54d-42d0-afb3-7b5071a7f6f8","uuid":"be943b47-eed0-43f7-a281-1e59fa41d2cf","timestamp":"2026-05-21T09:28:33.223Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T085919/worst/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"real_data_dif_mmlu\",\n    \"description\": \"MH-DIF on METABENCH MMLU 5219×12508, temporal/ability cohorts, Li et al. proxy validation, within-family control, domain specificity\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"temporal_ets_c_pct\": 31.3,\n      \"temporal_enrichment_li\": 0.96,\n      \"ability_ets_c_pct\": 2.9,\n      \"within_family_llama2v3_c_pct\": 61.3,\n      \"within_family_mistral_mixtral_c_pct\": 38.7,\n      \"domain_spearman_rho\": 0.307,\n      \"domain_spearman_p\": 0.02,\n      \"sanity_random_split_c_pct\": 0.18,\n      \"irt_misfit_pct_lz_gt2\": 98.0\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table 1: DIF classification by cohort type (temporal vs ability vs random sanity) — reviewer acknowledges but notes ability cohort C%=2.9% proves DIF is mainly detecting ability differences\",\n      \"Table 2: Within-family DIF rates — reviewer cites Llama-2v3 61.3% as proof that capability improvement dominates the signal\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"In the worst tier, plan_001 becomes the paper's biggest liability. The enrichment≈1.0 result, intended as support for the meta-critique, is flipped into evidence that DIF doesn't work. The within-family Llama-2v3 result, intended as a confound control, becomes Exhibit A for the \\\"it's just ability\\\" argument. The 98% IRT misfit is a pre-existing condition that poisons all downstream analyses. Reviewer concludes: \\\"The finding is either trivially explained (models improve) or produced by a misspecified model (IRT doesn't fit). Either way, the 31% number is not interpretable as claimed.\\\"\\n\\n\\nNumbers themselves are reproducible but reviewers in this tier attack interpretation, not arithmetic. (1) enrichment≈0.96 is used as primary evidence AGAINST DIF: \\\"your detector is no better than random relative to the only available ground truth — you've built a method that doesn't correlate with contamination.\\\" The nuanced meta-critique (web-overlap ≠ differential contamination) is dismissed as post-hoc rationalization. (2) 31.3% temporal C% vs 0.18% random sanity is acknowledged as non-random, but reviewer frames it as \\\"31% of items get easier when models get better — that's called progress, not instability.\\\" The ρ=0.307 domain correlation supports this reading. (3) Within-family Llama-2v3 at 61.3% is treated as a fatal confound: \\\"your biggest within-family comparison shows that capability gain alone produces massive DIF, confirming the 31% temporal finding is ability confounding.\\\" (4) 98% IRT |Lz|>2 triggers the strongest attack: \\\"your entire analysis rests on a model that fits almost no examinees — DIF from a misspecified model is uninterpretable. You should have started with multidimensional IRT or abandoned the psychometric framing entirely.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"rl_robustness_validation\",\n    \"description\": \"Validate DIF robustness to RL-trained models: SFT baseline, alignment-RL (DPO/ORPO), reasoning-RL (GRPO/PPO)\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_reasoning_rl_models_available\": 13,\n      \"sft_baseline_c_pct\": 30.8,\n      \"alignment_rl_added_c_pct\": 31.1,\n      \"reasoning_rl_added_c_pct\": 28.4,\n      \"jaccard_sft_vs_alignment_rl\": 0.72,\n      \"jaccard_sft_vs_reasoning_rl\": 0.58,\n      \"min_k_auroc_sft\": 0.61,\n      \"min_k_auroc_reasoning_rl\": 0.54,\n      \"constat_auroc_sft\": 0.58,\n      \"constat_auroc_reasoning_rl\": 0.49,\n      \"bootstrap_ci_delong_p\": 0.18,\n      \"tost_equivalence_p\": 0.09\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: DIF flag stability across cohort compositions — reviewer dismisses as underpowered\",\n      \"Table: Per-method AUROC with bootstrap CIs — reviewer notes overlapping CIs across all methods\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Plan_002 was already identified as underpowered (N=13) in the framing doc. In worst tier, it actively hurts the paper: including it signals that the authors are aware of the RL robustness question but can only muster 13 models. Better to have acknowledged the limitation than to have run a test that fails to convince. The contamination detection community reviewer treats this as the weakest section and uses it to question the authors' experimental rigor throughout.\\n\\n\\nN=13 reasoning-RL models is the core weakness. In worst tier, reviewers dismiss the entire experiment: \\\"You tested 13 models and claim robustness to RL? That's an anecdote, not validation.\\\" The Jaccard drop from 0.72 (alignment-RL) to 0.58 (reasoning-RL) is read as evidence of non-trivial instability, not robustness. DeLong test p=0.18 is non-significant but CI is wide — reviewer notes \\\"failure to reject is not evidence of equivalence, and your TOST at p=0.09 doesn't pass the conventional α=0.05 threshold.\\\" Traditional detector degradation (MinK% AUROC 0.61→0.54, ConStat 0.58→0.49) is modest, undermining the narrative that DIF uniquely resists RL. A contamination detection reviewer argues: \\\"You show marginal degradation for traditional methods on 13 models. My method tested on 500+ models shows no such issue. Your comparison is underpowered and misleading.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"constat_perturbation_eval\",\n    \"description\": \"Independent ConStat-style perturbation testing on 200+ MMLU items as baseline comparison\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_items_reformulated\": 220,\n      \"n_models_tested\": 45,\n      \"constat_detection_rate_overall\": 0.24,\n      \"constat_fdr_corrected_rate\": 0.18,\n      \"constat_vs_dif_overlap_jaccard\": 0.09,\n      \"constat_precision_vs_li_labels\": 0.42,\n      \"constat_recall_vs_li_labels\": 0.31,\n      \"effect_size_original_vs_reformulated\": 0.34\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: ConStat detection rates by MMLU domain — shows plausible domain variation\",\n      \"Table: DIF vs ConStat flag overlap matrix — Jaccard=0.09 highlighted as near-zero agreement\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"ConStat performing reasonably well is the worst-tier killer for Claim 2. The meta-critique (\\\"web-overlap labels can't validate item-level detectors\\\") loses force when ConStat shows non-trivial correlation with those same labels. The near-zero overlap with DIF shifts the burden of proof: reviewers ask DIF to explain why it disagrees with a method that correlates with ground truth, rather than asking ConStat to explain why it agrees with a flawed ground truth.\\n\\n\\nIn worst tier, ConStat actually performs respectably on the 220 items — 24% detection pre-FDR, 18% post-FDR. This creates a problem: if ConStat detects contamination at 18% and DIF detects instability at 31%, and they overlap at only Jaccard=0.09, reviewer asks \\\"which one is right?\\\" The low overlap is framed not as \\\"measuring different constructs\\\" (the paper's intended argument) but as \\\"at least one method has very high false positives.\\\" ConStat's 0.42 precision against Li et al. labels (vs DIF's enrichment≈1.0) is used to argue ConStat is the more valid detector: \\\"ConStat at least correlates with known contamination; DIF correlates with nothing.\\\" The modest effect size (0.34) on reformulations suggests genuine contamination signal exists and ConStat captures it. Reviewer from contamination detection community: \\\"This comparison validates our perturbation approach and shows DIF is measuring something else entirely — probably ability confounding, as we suspected.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"dvd_generation_variance_eval\",\n    \"description\": \"DVD generation variance analysis on 10-15 open-weight models × 50 generations per item\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_models_tested\": 12,\n      \"n_items_tested\": 500,\n      \"n_generations_per_item\": 50,\n      \"bimodal_detection_rate\": 0.07,\n      \"dvd_vs_dif_overlap_jaccard\": 0.05,\n      \"dvd_vs_constat_overlap_jaccard\": 0.11,\n      \"dvd_auroc_vs_li_labels\": 0.56,\n      \"temperature_sensitivity\": \"results vary >15% between T=0.7 and T=1.0\",\n      \"compute_cost_gpu_hours\": 68\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: DVD detection rates by model family — sparse, only 12 models\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"DVD adds little to the paper in any tier. In worst tier, it becomes dead weight: GPU-expensive, underpowered, and its results are used by reviewers to argue the field needs more careful method comparison, not another method (DIF). The three-way disagreement (DIF/ConStat/DVD all flag different items) is the worst possible outcome — it makes all methods look unreliable.\\n\\n\\nIn worst tier, DVD produces weak but non-zero results (7% bimodal items), and the near-zero overlap with DIF (Jaccard=0.05) reinforces the narrative that DIF is an outlier method. The 12 models tested are few enough that reviewer questions generalizability, but results are just good enough to suggest DVD captures a different contamination signal. Temperature sensitivity (>15% variation) is noted but not fatal — reviewer says \\\"this shows contamination detection is sensitive to evaluation protocol, which is interesting and under-explored in this paper.\\\" The 68 GPU-hours spent on this experiment is seen as disproportionate to its contribution, and the results feel like a method survey rather than a paper contribution. Reviewer: \\\"You've shown three methods disagree. That's not a contribution — that's a problem statement.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"ensemble_fusion\",\n    \"description\": \"Fuse DIF + ConStat + DVD into multi-signal ensemble with calibrated thresholds\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"majority_voting_precision\": 0.15,\n      \"majority_voting_recall\": 0.04,\n      \"weighted_fusion_auroc\": 0.59,\n      \"ensemble_fpr\": 0.12,\n      \"cross_validation_variance\": 0.08,\n      \"uniform_vs_optimized_delta_auroc\": 0.03,\n      \"n_items_flagged_by_all_three\": 28\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Ensemble performance under different fusion strategies — all near chance\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Ensemble was the original Claim 2 headline. Its failure in worst tier is predictable from the near-zero pairwise overlaps. The experiment serves as evidence that DIF, ConStat, and DVD are incommensurable — which could be an interesting finding but is framed as a failure rather than a discovery. Reviewer sees this as confirming the paper lacks a coherent detection contribution.\\n\\n\\nIn worst tier, ensemble fusion fails because the constituent signals disagree fundamentally (Jaccard <0.11 pairwise). Majority voting produces extremely low recall (0.04) because so few items are flagged by 2+ methods. Weighted fusion AUROC of 0.59 barely exceeds chance, and cross-validation variance of 0.08 means the weights are unstable. Only 28 items flagged by all three methods — too few to draw conclusions. Reviewer: \\\"The ensemble doesn't work because the methods measure different things. This is acknowledged in the paper but then the authors proceed to fuse them anyway, which is methodologically incoherent. If DIF measures 'instability' and ConStat measures 'contamination,' why would you ensemble them?\\\" The optimized weights barely improve over uniform (Δ=0.03), suggesting no meaningful signal structure in the combination. This experiment, dependent on 003a and 003b both working, collapses when its inputs disagree.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"cross_benchmark_gsm8k\",\n    \"description\": \"DIF replication on GSM8K (METABENCH) + GSM1K contamination gap correlation\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"gsm8k_n_items\": 1319,\n      \"gsm8k_temporal_c_pct\": 18.9,\n      \"semi_synthetic_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.818,\n      \"gsm1k_n_models\": 33,\n      \"gsm1k_pearson_r\": -0.478,\n      \"gsm1k_pearson_p\": 0.005,\n      \"gsm1k_spearman_rho\": -0.403,\n      \"gsm1k_95ci_upper_abs_r\": 0.155,\n      \"gsm1k_partial_r\": -0.385,\n      \"cooks_d_max\": \"Deepseek-math-7b-rl\",\n      \"leave_one_out_r_range\": \"[-0.537, -0.374]\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: GSM1K correlation with CIs — reviewer circles the CI upper bounds\",\n      \"Table: Leave-one-out sensitivity — shows stability but reviewer notes 'stable weak effect is still weak'\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Plan_004 Step 3 is the only cross-benchmark validation with an independent ground truth (GSM1K). In worst tier, N=33 and the difficulty confound make it suggestive rather than compelling. Reviewers don't attack the direction (negative r is actually more interesting than positive), but they attack the power and the alternative explanation. The semi-synthetic Step 2 (ΔTPR=0.818) is strong but dismissed as \\\"artificial\\\" (same attack as plan_011). Step 1 (C%=18.9% on GSM8K) is consistent with the MMLU finding but reviewer notes: \\\"Lower rate on a single-domain benchmark is expected — this doesn't validate the MMLU finding, it just shows DIF produces a number everywhere you run it.\\\"\\n\\n\\nStep 1 (GSM8K DIF) and Step 2 (semi-synthetic) are solid but in worst tier reviewers focus exclusively on Step 3 (GSM1K correlation). N=33 is the attack surface: \\\"33 models is a convenience sample, not a systematic evaluation.\\\" The BCa CI upper bound for |r| at 0.155 (Pearson) and 0.031 (Spearman) means \\\"the true effect could be negligibly small.\\\" The negative direction (DIF items RESIST contamination gains) is interesting but reviewers in worst tier flip it: \\\"So DIF items are just harder items that contamination doesn't help with? That's not evidence DIF detects contamination — it's evidence DIF detects difficulty.\\\" The item difficulty confound (DIF-C items are harder, Cohen's d=-0.247 from plan_023) directly supports this alternative explanation. Deepseek-math-7b-rl as the max Cook's D model raises eyebrows: \\\"Your strongest result depends on one model specifically designed for math reasoning.\\\" Reviewer: \\\"N=33, wide CIs, plausible difficulty confound, one influential outlier. This is exploratory at best.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"bifactor_irt_equating_pilot\",\n    \"description\": \"Bifactor IRT model on MMLU (general + subject factors), anchor item selection, fixed-parameter calibration\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"bifactor_convergence\": false,\n      \"rmsea\": \">0.10 (poor fit)\",\n      \"cfi\": \"<0.80 (poor fit)\",\n      \"n_items_attempted\": 12508,\n      \"n_subjects_as_factors\": 57,\n      \"estimation_time_hours\": 48,\n      \"anchor_items_identified\": 0,\n      \"equating_completed\": false\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Plan_005 is a known failure (killed as plan_015). In worst tier, reviewers cite it as evidence of methodological over-reach: the authors know IRT doesn't fit MMLU (98% |Lz|>2, bifactor fails) but continue to use IRT-derived MH-DIF. This is the most damaging self-inflicted wound in the paper. The planned equating (Claim 3 original) is dead. Even in the current framing where equating is dropped, a reviewer who notices the bifactor attempt will ask: \\\"If bifactor IRT can't model MMLU, why should we trust MH-DIF, which conditions on a unidimensional total score?\\\"\\n\\n\\nIn worst tier, the bifactor model fails to converge on 57 subject-specific factors + 1 general factor for 12,508 items. This is computationally expected — bifactor IRT with 58 latent dimensions and 12K items requires enormous sample sizes and careful specification. Even if forced to converge, RMSEA>0.10 and CFI<0.80 indicate poor fit, meaning the factor structure doesn't capture MMLU's complexity. No anchor items can be identified because the model doesn't fit, so equating never starts. This is consistent with the plan_015 outcome (killed at PREPARING stage). Reviewer: \\\"The authors attempted IRT equating but their model doesn't fit — this confirms IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU, which undermines their entire DIF analysis (which also rests on IRT assumptions). They acknowledge this but proceed anyway.\\\" The failure is self-damaging: it provides direct evidence against the paper's own methodology.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"item_generation_calibration\",\n    \"description\": \"LLM-generated replacement items for DIF-flagged questions, calibrated via student simulation\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_items_generated\": 300,\n      \"difficulty_correlation_prompt_based\": 0.18,\n      \"difficulty_correlation_student_sim\": 0.52,\n      \"target_difficulty_mae\": 0.21,\n      \"construct_coverage_maintained\": false,\n      \"holdout_model_panel_rho\": 0.72,\n      \"expert_quality_rating\": \"2.8/5 (below acceptable threshold)\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Difficulty correlation by calibration method — both below targets\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Plan_006 depends on plan_005 (bifactor equating) which already failed. Without a working equating framework, replacement items cannot be properly calibrated. In worst tier, this experiment embodies the reviewer critique \\\"interesting observation, but what do we do with it?\\\" — the answer is \\\"we tried to do something, and it didn't work well enough.\\\" This reinforces the purely-descriptive characterization of the paper.\\n\\n\\nIn worst tier, item generation fails at the calibration stage. Prompt-based difficulty targeting (ρ=0.18) is near the lower bound of published estimates (0.10-0.45). Student simulation improves to ρ=0.52 but remains well below the r=0.75-0.82 reported in the source paper (arXiv 2601.09953), likely because MMLU's 57-domain breadth is harder to simulate than narrow-domain tests. Generated items don't maintain construct coverage: reviewers note that LLM-generated questions cluster in easily-generatable knowledge areas, leaving specialized domains under-represented. The holdout validation (ρ=0.72) is below the 0.95 threshold specified in the plan. Reviewer: \\\"The authors can't even generate replacement items that correlate above 0.75 with the originals. This undermines the practical value of detecting unstable items — if you can't replace them, knowing they're unstable is academic.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"cumulative_bayesian_dif\",\n    \"description\": \"Bayesian DIF priors (Sinharay et al. 2006) across 4 simulated benchmark administrations\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"single_admin_power_d10\": 0.38,\n      \"cumulative_2_admin_power_d10\": 0.49,\n      \"cumulative_3_admin_power_d10\": 0.58,\n      \"cumulative_4_admin_power_d10\": 0.64,\n      \"target_power_0_70_achieved\": false,\n      \"large_effect_d14_cumulative_4\": 0.73,\n      \"simulation_only\": true,\n      \"real_data_validation\": \"none\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Power by cumulative administrations and effect size — incremental gains flatten\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Plan_007 is pure simulation with no empirical anchor. In worst tier, it is the most expendable section — reviewers see it as speculative future work masquerading as a result. The 8-page constraint means including this pushes out content that could address real reviewer concerns. Framing doc already marked this as appendix material; in worst tier, even appendix inclusion is questioned as padding.\\n\\n\\nIn worst tier, the Bayesian cumulative approach shows improvement over single administrations but falls short of the target 0.70 power for medium effects (δ=1.0) after 4 administrations — reaching only 0.64. This is because the MMLU item parameters used in the simulation reflect the misspecified 1D IRT model, propagating bias into the Bayesian priors. For large effects (δ=1.4), cumulative power of 0.73 barely clears the threshold, but reviewers note: \\\"You already showed in phase 2 that single-admin MH achieves 0.68 at δ=1.4 — the Bayesian approach adds massive complexity for 5 percentage points.\\\" The simulation-only nature (no real multi-wave benchmark data exists) is fatal: \\\"This is a simulation study within a simulation — you simulated items from a misspecified model, then simulated multiple administrations that don't exist. There are no real data to validate any of this.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"graceful_degradation_analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Benchmark validity degradation under progressive item replacement (5-50%)\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"rho_5pct_replacement\": 0.998,\n      \"rho_10pct_replacement\": 0.995,\n      \"rho_20pct_replacement\": 0.988,\n      \"rho_50pct_replacement\": 0.962,\n      \"reliability_drop_at_31pct\": 0.006,\n      \"construct_coverage_loss_at_31pct\": \"4 of 57 subjects lose >50% items\",\n      \"validity_lower_bound_analytical\": 0.94\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Ranking stability metrics by replacement rate — all high\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Graceful degradation is a double-edged sword. In best tiers, it supports the \\\"instability ≠ invalidity\\\" framing as nuanced. In worst tier, it's used to argue the entire paper investigates a non-problem. Reviewer: \\\"MMLU tolerates 50% item replacement with ρ>0.96. Your 31% instability finding is interesting but inconsequential. The benchmark works fine. Move on.\\\"\\n\\n\\nIn worst tier, graceful degradation actually works TOO well, undermining the paper's significance. ρ=0.988 at 20% replacement and 0.962 at 50% shows MMLU is massively redundant. Reviewers use this against Claim 1: \\\"You've shown that removing 31% of items barely moves rankings (ρ≈0.997 from plan_017). You've now shown that replacing up to 50% of items only drops ρ to 0.962. So the 31% instability doesn't matter — the benchmark is robust to it. Why should anyone care?\\\" The 4 subjects losing >50% items at 31% replacement is a minor concern but reviewer notes these are small subjects (N<50 items) already statistically unreliable. This experiment provides ammunition for the \\\"instability ≠ invalidity\\\" framing but reviewers in worst tier turn it into \\\"instability AND invalidity are both non-issues, so what's the contribution?\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"item_focused_trees\",\n    \"description\": \"Item-Focused Trees (Tutz & Berger 2016) — DIF with continuous release-date covariate\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_items_with_significant_splits\": 3421,\n      \"pct_items_with_splits\": 27.4,\n      \"concordance_with_mh_dif_c\": 0.61,\n      \"primary_split_variable\": \"release_date\",\n      \"secondary_splits\": \"model_size (19%), training_data_cutoff (12%)\",\n      \"tree_depth_median\": 2,\n      \"overfitting_cv_auc_drop\": 0.14\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Split variable importance across items — release_date dominates\",\n      \"Table: MH vs IFT flag concordance matrix\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Item-Focused Trees are a robustness check that in worst tier becomes a liability. The moderate MH concordance and the model_size secondary splits give reviewers a clean alternative narrative: \\\"DIF detects where models improved, which correlates with when they were released and how big they are. This is capability scaling, observable from accuracy trends alone, and requires no psychometric machinery.\\\" The IFT results paradoxically make the simpler explanation (capability growth) more plausible than the DIF interpretation (item-level functional instability).\\n\\n\\nIn worst tier, Item-Focused Trees reveal that model release date is the primary split for 27.4% of items — consistent with temporal DIF but concordance with MH (0.61) is moderate, not high. This opens an attack: \\\"If a more flexible method agrees with MH on only 61% of flags, MH is either over- or under-detecting.\\\" Secondary splits on model_size (19%) and training_data_cutoff (12%) suggest DIF is partially confounded with these covariates, not purely temporal. The cv_auc drop of 0.14 indicates overfitting, which reviewers use to question tree-based results. Most damagingly, reviewer notes: \\\"If release date is the primary DIF driver and model size is secondary, you're detecting capability scaling, not contamination or instability. These trees just rediscover that bigger, newer models do better on certain items.\\\" The continuous-covariate approach, intended to avoid arbitrary cohort splits, instead reveals that the underlying signal is smooth capability growth, not discrete item-level dysfunction.\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"architecture_stratified_dif\",\n    \"description\": \"DIF within decoder-only subpopulation to test IRT fit improvement when architecture is homogeneous\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_decoder_only\": 5226,\n      \"n_non_decoder\": 1,\n      \"pct_decoder_only\": 99.98,\n      \"c_pct_decoder_only\": 31.3,\n      \"c_pct_full\": 31.3,\n      \"jaccard_decoder_only_vs_full\": 1.0,\n      \"irt_rmsea_decoder_only\": 0.089,\n      \"irt_rmsea_full\": 0.091,\n      \"irt_misfit_pct_decoder_only\": 97.8,\n      \"fit_improvement\": \"negligible\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: Architecture composition of METABENCH — one row, one number\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Plan_010 was designed as a proactive defense against the IRT misfit concern. In worst tier, it proves the defense is irrelevant: the IRT misfit isn't from architecture heterogeneity (which doesn't exist) but from MMLU's inherent multidimensionality (which can't be fixed by subsetting). The experiment answers the wrong question and wastes precious page space on a tautological result. Reviewer correctly identifies that the real issue — multidimensional IRT — is acknowledged but never attempted.\\n\\n\\nIn worst tier, this experiment backfires. The finding that METABENCH MMLU is 99.98% decoder-only (5226/5227) means the architecture stratification is trivial — removing one model changes nothing (Jaccard=1.0). Reviewer: \\\"This isn't an experiment, it's a data description. You showed your dataset has one non-decoder model. The fact that you needed a whole experiment to discover this suggests insufficient dataset exploration before running analyses.\\\" More importantly, IRT misfit barely improves (97.8% vs 98.0%) in the decoder-only subset, confirming that architecture heterogeneity was never the source of misfit — multidimensionality is. This should have been the paper's primary concern from the start. Reviewer: \\\"Architecture homogeneity doesn't help because the problem is 57 subject domains, not architecture. You've ruled out the wrong confounder.\\\"\\n\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"semi_synthetic_contamination_validation\",\n    \"description\": \"Inject known contamination into METABENCH response matrix, measure DIF detection power\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"n_clean_items_selected\": 500,\n      \"n_items_injected\": 200,\n      \"temporal_external_delta_tpr_p05\": 0.508,\n      \"temporal_external_delta_tpr_p10\": 0.674,\n      \"temporal_external_fpr\": 0.314,\n      \"dose_response_p03_tpr\": 0.548,\n      \"dose_response_p07_tpr\": 0.99,\n      \"dose_response_p10_tpr\": 1.0,\n      \"fpr_inflation_standard_mh\": \"0.298→0.616 at p=1.0\",\n      \"matching_variable_contamination\": true\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table: ΔTPR dose-response curve — reviewer acknowledges monotonicity but questions mechanism\",\n      \"Table: FPR inflation under standard vs external matching — reviewer focuses on baseline FPR\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Plan_011 is the paper's strongest controlled experiment, but in worst tier it's undermined by three attacks: (1) injection mechanism unrealistic, (2) baseline FPR=31% meaning the method flags ~1/3 of clean items, (3) matching variable contamination shows fragility. The 31% baseline FPR is the same 31% temporal C% from plan_001 — reviewer connects these: \\\"Your 'semi-synthetic clean items' still show 31% DIF because your method has a 31% base rate. Injection just adds more flags on top. The dose-response proves injection works as a perturbation, not that DIF detects contamination.\\\" This is the most sophisticated attack in the worst tier — it reframes the entire semi-synthetic framework as measuring perturbation sensitivity, not contamination detection.\\n\\nIn worst tier, the semi-synthetic results are numerically strong (ΔTPR=+0.508, clear dose-response) but reviewers attack the injection mechanism itself. \\\"Flipping incorrect→correct with probability p is a caricature of contamination. Real contamination manifests through training data exposure, which can produce partial memorization, template matching, or reasoning shortcuts — none of which look like a coin flip.\\\" The baseline FPR of 0.314 (31.4% of clean items flagged before injection) is treated as disqualifying: \\\"Your method has a 31% false positive rate on items you selected as clean. Any TPR increase is measured against an already-noisy baseline.\\\" The FPR inflation of standard MH (0.298→0.616) when injection contaminates the matching variable is noted but reviewer argues: \\\"This means your method is fragile to exactly the kind of correlated contamination that occurs in practice.\\\" The contamination detection community reviewer: \\\"Semi-synthetic injection is the weakest form of validation — we stopped using it years ago in favor of real contamination benchmarks. This is validation theater.\\\"\\n\"\n  }\n]","numLines":262,"startLine":1,"totalLines":262}}}
{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01UfBdigQUuApR97pj24RVvT","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I have a thorough understanding of the experiment data and paper framing. This is a \"worst\" tier simulation — meaning I need to write a paper that reflects the weaknesses described in the narrative guidance. The reviewers will see this as:\n\n1. MH-DIF as textbook psychometrics applied to a matrix — engineering, not research\n2. 31% figure dismissed as ability confounding + IRT misspecification artifact\n3. Meta-critique seen as an attack on contamination detection community\n4. Paper seen as descriptive catalog with no actionable contribution\n5. 8-page compression makes everything superficial\n6. One defensive contamination detection community reviewer\n   \nI'll structure a complete LaTeX paper that incorporates all 12 experiments from the data, using the actual numbers to ground the analysis while letting the framing and compression naturally create the weaknesses the reviewers will identify. The paper will present the experiments systematically but struggle to synthesize them into a coherent narrative or actionable insight. I'm going to structure this as a worst-tier paper that tries to cram all thirteen experiments into the 8-page limit, which will naturally create that superficial, catalog-like feel with the framing issues we discussed.\n\nWriting the LaTeX paper...","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":6318,"cache_creation_input_tokens":8959,"cache_read_input_tokens":24013,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":8959},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"776d36a2-b54d-42d0-afb3-7b5071a7f6f8","uuid":"a1996aa6-67b8-4ec6-a71e-57db0d02ec96"}
