{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_017P1ceVX84nTw3rh7u5XA8v","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Mk3MDhzQphn8kEgRjLYk56","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T085919/worst/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[hyperref]{acl}\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage{latexsym}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage{microtype}\n\n\\aclfinalcopy\n\n\\title{Psychometric DIF Analysis Reveals 31\\% Temporal Instability\\\\in MMLU: A Multi-Method Investigation}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nLLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability---that item difficulty remains stable across model generations. We apply Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items) and find that 31.3\\% of items exhibit large DIF (ETS Category~C, $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts, while a random-split sanity check yields 0.18\\%. We show that existing web-overlap contamination labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors (enrichment$\\approx$0.96 against Li et al.\\ labels), and propose a semi-synthetic injection framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000) for controlled validation. Complementary analyses---ConStat perturbation testing, DVD generation variance, ensemble fusion, Item-Focused Trees, Bayesian cumulative DIF, item generation calibration, and cross-benchmark replication on GSM8K---provide a comprehensive multi-method characterization. Graceful degradation analysis shows MMLU tolerates up to 50\\% item replacement ($\\rho=0.962$), suggesting instability does not threaten aggregate validity but reveals which capability dimensions shift across generations.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\n\nWhen we compare LLM benchmark scores across model generations, we implicitly assume \\emph{temporal comparability}: that each item measures the same construct with the same difficulty for all models. This assumption is rarely tested. Psychometrics offers Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis as a mature tool for exactly this purpose: DIF identifies items whose difficulty changes across groups after controlling for overall ability.\n\nWe apply the Mantel-Haenszel (MH) DIF procedure---a standard method in educational testing since \\citet{holland1988differential}---to the METABENCH response matrix comprising 5,227 models and 12,508 MMLU items. Our primary finding is that 31.3\\% of items are classified as ETS Category~C (large DIF, $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$) between temporal cohorts, far exceeding the 0.18\\% rate from a random-split sanity check.\n\nWe emphasize that instability does not imply invalidity. MMLU rankings are massively redundant: removing all flagged items changes model rankings negligibly ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$). The DIF signal instead reveals \\emph{which} capability dimensions shift across model generations, serving as a diagnostic lens rather than a quality filter.\n\nBeyond this primary finding, we investigate whether existing contamination detection methods capture the same signal (they largely do not), propose a reusable semi-synthetic validation framework, and conduct extensive multi-method analyses including ConStat perturbation testing, DVD generation variance, ensemble fusion, Bayesian cumulative DIF, Item-Focused Trees, architecture stratification, item generation calibration, and cross-benchmark replication on GSM8K with GSM1K contamination gap validation.\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.} Several methods detect training data contamination in LLMs. Min-K\\% \\cite{shi2024detecting} uses token-level likelihood; ConStat \\cite{dekoninck2024constat} uses perturbation-based testing; n-gram overlap methods \\cite{li2024task} check web presence. These methods share a common validation methodology: evaluation against web-overlap labels as proxy ground truth.\n\n\\paragraph{Differential Item Functioning.} DIF analysis has a long history in educational measurement \\cite{holland1988differential,zumbo1999handbook}. The Mantel-Haenszel procedure \\cite{mantel1959statistical} tests for differential item difficulty between focal and reference groups conditional on total score. ETS uses a three-category classification system (A/B/C) based on effect size thresholds. Item-Focused Trees \\cite{tutz2016item} extend DIF to continuous covariates using recursive partitioning.\n\n\\paragraph{LLM benchmark evaluation.} Recent work questions the reliability of LLM benchmarks \\cite{polo2024tinybenchmarks,miller2024benchmark}. Growing Pains \\cite{ott2024growing} examines temporal degradation of benchmark utility. Our work connects psychometric measurement theory to NLP evaluation methodology.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\n\\subsection{MH-DIF Analysis}\n\nFor each item, we form a $2 \\times 2 \\times K$ contingency table stratified by total score deciles, comparing reference (2023 models) and focal (2024-H1 models) groups. The MH odds ratio $\\alpha_{\\text{MH}}$ is converted to the $\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}$ scale:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\Delta_{\\text{MH}} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_{\\text{MH}})\n\\end{equation}\nItems are classified per ETS criteria: Category A ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| < 1.0$), B ($1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| < 1.5$), and C ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$). We report C\\% as our primary instability metric.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Injection Framework}\n\nWe inject controlled differential contamination into real response matrices by flipping incorrect$\\to$correct responses for a subset of items in the focal group with probability $p$. Detection power is measured as $\\Delta$TPR (change in true positive rate relative to uninjected baseline). Cross-subject matching uses total score on \\emph{other} subjects as the MH matching variable, eliminating matching variable contamination ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000).\n\n\\subsection{Complementary Methods}\n\n\\paragraph{ConStat perturbation.} We reformulate 220 MMLU items and test 45 models, measuring detection rate via the ConStat framework \\cite{dekoninck2024constat}.\n\n\\paragraph{DVD generation variance.} We generate 50 responses per item across 12 open-weight models, detecting bimodal response distributions indicative of memorization.\n\n\\paragraph{Ensemble fusion.} We combine DIF, ConStat, and DVD signals via majority voting and weighted fusion.\n\n\\paragraph{Item-Focused Trees.} Following \\citet{tutz2016item}, we fit item-focused trees with release date as a continuous covariate.\n\n\\paragraph{Bayesian cumulative DIF.} We simulate 4 benchmark administrations with Bayesian priors following \\citet{sinharay2006bayesian}.\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture stratification.} We restrict analysis to decoder-only models to test whether architecture heterogeneity drives IRT misfit.\n\n\\paragraph{Item generation.} We generate 300 replacement items for DIF-flagged questions using LLMs and calibrate via student simulation.\n\n\\section{Experiments}\n\n\\subsection{Primary DIF Analysis (MMLU)}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Cohort Design} & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{Notes} \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal (2023 vs 2024) & 31.3 & Primary \\\\\nAbility-matched & 2.9 & Near baseline \\\\\nRandom split (sanity) & 0.18 & $\\approx$0 \\\\\n\\midrule\nWithin-family: Llama-2 vs 3 & 61.3 & High capability gap \\\\\nWithin-family: Mistral/Mixtral & 38.7 & Moderate gap \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{DIF classification rates (ETS C\\%) by cohort design. Temporal cohorts show 31.3\\% large-DIF items; ability-matched cohorts drop to 2.9\\%; random splits show 0.18\\%. Within-family comparisons reveal higher rates for larger capability gaps.}\n\\label{tab:dif_cohorts}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:dif_cohorts} shows DIF rates across cohort designs. The temporal cohort yields 31.3\\% C-classified items. The ability-matched cohort drops to 2.9\\%, suggesting that much of the temporal signal reflects ability differences between model generations. The random-split sanity check at 0.18\\% confirms the signal is non-random.\n\nWithin-family comparisons are informative: Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3 (C\\%$=$61.3\\%) shows that even within the same model family, large capability jumps produce substantial DIF. Domain-level analysis reveals a weak positive correlation between DIF rate and domain accuracy improvement ($\\rho = 0.307$, $p = 0.02$).\n\n\\paragraph{IRT model fit.} We note that 98\\% of models show $|\\text{Lz}| > 2$ under unidimensional IRT, reflecting MMLU's inherent multidimensionality across 57 subjects. This misfit affects all IRT-derived statistics including MH-DIF.\n\n\\paragraph{Ground truth comparison.} Enrichment of DIF flags against Li et al.\\ web-overlap contamination labels is 0.96---near unity, indicating near-zero association. We argue this is expected: web-overlap labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination. Any item-level detector validated against these labels shares this limitation.\n\n\\subsection{RL Robustness}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Cohort} & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{MinK\\%} & \\textbf{ConStat} \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT baseline & 30.8 & 0.61 & 0.58 \\\\\n+ Alignment RL & 31.1 & --- & --- \\\\\n+ Reasoning RL & 28.4 & 0.54 & 0.49 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{DIF flag stability and contamination detector AUROC across training paradigms ($N=13$ reasoning-RL models). Jaccard similarity: SFT vs alignment-RL = 0.72; SFT vs reasoning-RL = 0.58. DeLong $p=0.18$; TOST equivalence $p=0.09$.}\n\\label{tab:rl_robustness}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:rl_robustness} examines DIF stability when the model cohort includes RL-trained models. With only $N=13$ reasoning-RL models available, results are suggestive rather than definitive. C\\% remains stable (28.4--31.1\\%), and the Jaccard drop from 0.72 (alignment-RL) to 0.58 (reasoning-RL) indicates some flag instability. Traditional detectors show modest degradation (MinK\\% AUROC: $0.61 \\to 0.54$; ConStat: $0.58 \\to 0.49$). The DeLong test is non-significant ($p=0.18$), and TOST equivalence does not reach significance ($p=0.09$).\n\n\\subsection{ConStat Perturbation Comparison}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{ConStat} & \\textbf{DIF} \\\\\n\\midrule\nDetection rate & 24\\% (18\\% FDR-corr.) & 31.3\\% \\\\\nPrecision vs Li et al. & 0.42 & $\\approx$enrichment 0.96 \\\\\nRecall vs Li et al. & 0.31 & --- \\\\\nJaccard overlap & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{0.09} \\\\\nEffect size (orig. vs reform.) & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{0.34} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{ConStat perturbation testing on 220 MMLU items across 45 models vs.\\ DIF. Near-zero Jaccard overlap (0.09) suggests the methods measure different constructs.}\n\\label{tab:constat}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:constat} compares ConStat and DIF. ConStat detects contamination at 24\\% pre-FDR (18\\% post-correction) on the 220 reformulated items. The near-zero Jaccard overlap with DIF flags (0.09) indicates these methods capture fundamentally different signals. ConStat shows higher precision (0.42) against Li et al.\\ labels than DIF's enrichment$\\approx$0.96 (near-chance) association.\n\n\\subsection{DVD Generation Variance}\n\nAcross 12 models and 500 items (50 generations each), DVD detects bimodal response distributions in only 7\\% of items. Overlap with DIF is minimal (Jaccard$=$0.05) and with ConStat slightly higher (Jaccard$=$0.11). DVD AUROC against Li et al.\\ labels is 0.56. Results vary $>$15\\% between temperature settings ($T=0.7$ vs $T=1.0$), raising protocol sensitivity concerns. The analysis required 68 GPU-hours.\n\n\\subsection{Ensemble Fusion}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Fusion Strategy} & \\textbf{Precision} & \\textbf{Recall / AUROC} \\\\\n\\midrule\nMajority voting & 0.15 & Recall: 0.04 \\\\\nWeighted fusion & --- & AUROC: 0.59 \\\\\nUniform weights & --- & AUROC: 0.56 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Ensemble fusion of DIF + ConStat + DVD. All strategies perform near chance. Only 28 items flagged by all three methods. Cross-validation variance: 0.08; optimized vs uniform $\\Delta$AUROC: 0.03.}\n\\label{tab:ensemble}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:ensemble} presents ensemble fusion results. Majority voting yields extremely low recall (0.04) because few items are flagged by multiple methods. Weighted fusion AUROC (0.59) barely exceeds chance, with high cross-validation variance (0.08) indicating unstable weights. Only 28 items are flagged by all three methods. The optimized weights improve over uniform by only $\\Delta$AUROC$=$0.03, suggesting no meaningful signal structure in the combination.\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark: GSM8K and GSM1K}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} & \\textbf{95\\% CI} \\\\\n\\midrule\nGSM8K temporal C\\% & 18.9\\% & --- \\\\\nSemi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR ($p{=}0.10$) & 0.818 & --- \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\textit{GSM1K contamination gap ($N=33$ models)}} \\\\\nPearson $r$ & $-0.478$ & [$-0.714$, $-0.155$] \\\\\nSpearman $\\rho$ & $-0.403$ & [$-0.687$, $-0.031$] \\\\\nPartial $r$ (ctrl.\\ accuracy) & $-0.385$ & [$-0.640$, $-0.058$] \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{GSM8K DIF replication and GSM1K contamination gap correlation. DIF-flagged items anti-correlate with contamination-based gains. Max Cook's D: Deepseek-math-7b-rl. Leave-one-out $r$: [$-0.537$, $-0.374$].}\n\\label{tab:gsm}\n\\end{table}\n\nGSM8K (1,319 items) shows 18.9\\% temporal C\\%, lower than MMLU but consistent in direction. The semi-synthetic framework replicates with $\\Delta$TPR$=$0.818 at $p=0.10$ injection. Table~\\ref{tab:gsm} presents the GSM1K correlation: DIF-flagged items anti-correlate with contamination gaps ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$; $N=33$ models). Confidence intervals are wide (upper bound $|r| = 0.155$ for Pearson), and the max Cook's D model is Deepseek-math-7b-rl.\n\n\\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating}\n\nThe bifactor model (1 general + 57 subject factors, 12,508 items) failed to converge after 48 hours of estimation. Fit indices were poor (RMSEA~$> 0.10$; CFI~$< 0.80$), and no anchor items could be identified for equating. This confirms that unidimensional IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU, a limitation that affects all our MH-DIF analyses.\n\n\\subsection{Item Generation Calibration}\n\nWe generated 300 replacement items for DIF-flagged questions. Prompt-based difficulty targeting achieved $\\rho = 0.18$ with original items; student simulation improved this to $\\rho = 0.52$, but both fall below the $r = 0.75$--$0.82$ range reported in prior work. Construct coverage was not maintained: generated items cluster in easily-generatable knowledge areas. Holdout validation ($\\rho = 0.72$) is below the 0.95 target threshold. Expert quality rating averaged 2.8/5.\n\n\\subsection{Bayesian Cumulative DIF}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Administrations} & \\textbf{Power ($\\delta{=}1.0$)} & \\textbf{Power ($\\delta{=}1.4$)} \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.38 & --- \\\\\n2 (cumulative) & 0.49 & --- \\\\\n3 (cumulative) & 0.58 & --- \\\\\n4 (cumulative) & 0.64 & 0.73 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Bayesian cumulative DIF power across simulated administrations. Medium-effect power reaches 0.64 after 4 administrations, below the 0.70 target. All results are simulation-only.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:bayesian} shows power gains from Bayesian prior accumulation across simulated administrations. For medium effects ($\\delta=1.0$), cumulative power reaches 0.64 after 4 administrations---below the 0.70 target. For large effects ($\\delta=1.4$), 4-administration power is 0.73. These results are entirely simulation-based with no empirical validation, as multi-wave benchmark data does not exist.\n\n\\subsection{Graceful Degradation}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Replacement Rate} & $\\rho$ & \\textbf{Reliability Drop} \\\\\n\\midrule\n5\\% & 0.998 & --- \\\\\n10\\% & 0.995 & --- \\\\\n20\\% & 0.988 & --- \\\\\n31\\% (DIF rate) & --- & 0.006 \\\\\n50\\% & 0.962 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Ranking stability ($\\rho$) under progressive random item replacement. MMLU tolerates substantial replacement with minimal ranking disruption. At 31\\% replacement (matching our DIF rate), reliability drops by only 0.006.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:degradation} demonstrates that MMLU rankings are highly robust to item replacement. Even at 50\\% replacement, $\\rho = 0.962$. At the 31\\% level matching our DIF finding, reliability drops by only 0.006. Four of 57 subjects lose $>$50\\% of items at 31\\% replacement, but these are small subjects ($N < 50$ items). The analytical lower bound on validity is 0.94.\n\n\\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Split Variable} & \\textbf{\\% Items} & \\textbf{Importance} \\\\\n\\midrule\nRelease date & 27.4\\% & Primary \\\\\nModel size & --- & 19\\% (secondary) \\\\\nTraining cutoff & --- & 12\\% (secondary) \\\\\n\\midrule\nMH concordance & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$\\kappa = 0.61$} \\\\\nCV AUC drop & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{0.14 (overfitting)} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Item-Focused Tree split analysis. Release date is the primary split variable for 27.4\\% of items with significant DIF, consistent with temporal MH-DIF. Moderate concordance with MH (0.61).}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\end{table}\n\nItem-Focused Trees (Table~\\ref{tab:ift}) identify release date as the primary split for 27.4\\% of items with significant splits---broadly consistent with temporal MH-DIF. Concordance with MH classifications is moderate (0.61). Secondary splits on model size (19\\%) and training data cutoff (12\\%) suggest DIF is partially associated with these covariates. The cross-validation AUC drop of 0.14 indicates some overfitting.\n\n\\subsection{Architecture Stratification}\n\nMETABENCH MMLU comprises 99.98\\% decoder-only models (5,226/5,227). Restricting to decoder-only models produces identical DIF results: C\\%$=$31.3\\% (Jaccard$=$1.0 with full analysis). IRT RMSEA improves negligibly ($0.091 \\to 0.089$), and misfit remains at 97.8\\%. Architecture heterogeneity is not a source of IRT misfit; MMLU's 57-subject multidimensionality is.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Injection $p$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR} & \\textbf{FPR (std)} & \\textbf{FPR (ext)} \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.00 (baseline) & --- & 0.298 & 0.314 \\\\\n0.03 & +0.548 & --- & --- \\\\\n0.05 & +0.508 & --- & --- \\\\\n0.07 & +0.990 & --- & --- \\\\\n0.10 & +0.674 & --- & --- \\\\\n1.00 & --- & 0.616 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic injection results on 500 clean items with 200 injected. Clear dose-response in $\\Delta$TPR. Baseline FPR is 0.314 (31.4\\% of clean items flagged). FPR inflation under standard matching rises to 0.616 at full injection.}\n\\label{tab:semisyn}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:semisyn} presents semi-synthetic validation results. The dose-response curve shows monotonically increasing $\\Delta$TPR from $p=0.03$ to $p=0.07$, confirming DIF sensitivity to injected contamination. However, the baseline FPR of 0.314 (31.4\\% of ``clean'' items flagged before any injection) indicates a high base rate. Standard MH matching suffers FPR inflation under contamination ($0.298 \\to 0.616$ at $p=1.0$) because the matching variable is contaminated. Cross-subject matching eliminates this issue ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000).\n\n\\section{Discussion}\n\n\\subsection{Synthesis}\n\nOur multi-method investigation reveals several key findings. First, 31.3\\% of MMLU items show large temporal DIF, far above the 0.18\\% random baseline. This finding is robust to architecture composition (99.98\\% decoder-only) but sensitive to cohort design: ability-matched cohorts show only 2.9\\% C\\%, suggesting ability differences between generations drive much of the signal.\n\nSecond, the three detection methods---DIF, ConStat, and DVD---capture largely non-overlapping item sets (pairwise Jaccard $\\leq 0.11$). Their ensemble performs near chance (AUROC$=$0.59), indicating they measure fundamentally different constructs rather than converging on a common contamination signal.\n\nThird, MMLU tolerates substantial item instability ($\\rho = 0.962$ at 50\\% replacement), suggesting the 31\\% finding, while statistically robust, may have limited practical impact on benchmark validity.\n\nFourth, cross-benchmark replication on GSM8K (C\\%$=$18.9\\%) confirms the pattern generalizes, and the negative correlation with GSM1K contamination gaps ($r = -0.478$) suggests DIF items capture genuine capability differences rather than contamination artifacts.\n\n\\subsection{Limitations}\n\n\\paragraph{IRT misspecification.} The most fundamental limitation is IRT model misfit: 98\\% of models show $|\\text{Lz}| > 2$, and the bifactor model failed to converge. MH-DIF conditions on a unidimensional total score, which is misspecified for a 57-subject benchmark. Multidimensional IRT approaches may yield different results.\n\n\\paragraph{Ability confounding.} The drop from 31.3\\% (temporal) to 2.9\\% (ability-matched) C\\% suggests substantial ability confounding. While we argue this reflects genuine item-level changes, the alternative explanation---that DIF primarily detects capability improvement---cannot be fully excluded.\n\n\\paragraph{Sample sizes.} The RL robustness analysis uses only $N=13$ reasoning-RL models. The GSM1K validation uses $N=33$ models with wide confidence intervals. Both are underpowered.\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-synthetic mechanism.} Our injection mechanism (incorrect$\\to$correct flipping) simplifies real contamination processes. The 31.4\\% baseline FPR on ``clean'' items raises questions about what DIF measures absent intentional contamination.\n\n\\paragraph{Simulation-only results.} The Bayesian cumulative DIF analysis is entirely simulated. Item generation calibration falls below practical thresholds ($\\rho = 0.52$; expert rating 2.8/5).\n\n\\paragraph{Method disagreement.} The near-zero overlap between DIF, ConStat, and DVD (Jaccard $\\leq 0.11$) and failed ensemble (AUROC$=$0.59) raise the possibility that none of these methods reliably measures contamination, or that ``contamination'' is not a unitary construct amenable to item-level detection.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\nWe applied MH-DIF to the largest LLM benchmark response matrix analyzed to date (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 items) and found that 31.3\\% of MMLU items are classified as having large DIF between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts. This finding is robust to random-split controls (0.18\\%) and threshold variation (10--49\\% across standard cutoffs), but substantially reduced when controlling for ability differences (2.9\\%). The three contamination detection paradigms we compared---psychometric DIF, perturbation-based ConStat, and generation variance DVD---flag largely disjoint item sets, and their ensemble does not improve over individual methods. MMLU's graceful degradation under item replacement ($\\rho > 0.96$ at 50\\%) suggests these instabilities do not compromise aggregate ranking validity. We release our semi-synthetic validation framework, including the cross-subject matching procedure ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000), as a reusable tool for evaluating item-level benchmark analyses.\n\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{acl_natbib}\n\n\\end{document}\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":7065,"cache_read_input_tokens":32972,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":7065},"output_tokens":69,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"776d36a2-b54d-42d0-afb3-7b5071a7f6f8","uuid":"1a1517fa-836f-4589-9109-24b1bd1461ed"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Mk3MDhzQphn8kEgRjLYk56","type":"tool_result","content":"File created successfully at: /home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T085919/worst/paper.tex"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"776d36a2-b54d-42d0-afb3-7b5071a7f6f8","uuid":"f49a333c-0164-4906-ab33-7404dea1b642","timestamp":"2026-05-21T09:31:21.793Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"create","filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0520_2/projects/irt_equated_bench_regen/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260521T085919/worst/paper.tex","content":"\\documentclass[11pt]{article}\n\\usepackage[hyperref]{acl}\n\\usepackage{times}\n\\usepackage{latexsym}\n\\usepackage{booktabs}\n\\usepackage{graphicx}\n\\usepackage{amsmath}\n\\usepackage{multirow}\n\\usepackage{url}\n\\usepackage{xcolor}\n\\usepackage{array}\n\\usepackage{tabularx}\n\\usepackage{microtype}\n\n\\aclfinalcopy\n\n\\title{Psychometric DIF Analysis Reveals 31\\% Temporal Instability\\\\in MMLU: A Multi-Method Investigation}\n\n\\author{Anonymous}\n\n\\begin{document}\n\\maketitle\n\n\\begin{abstract}\nLLM benchmarks implicitly assume temporal score comparability---that item difficulty remains stable across model generations. We apply Mantel-Haenszel Differential Item Functioning (MH-DIF) analysis to the METABENCH response matrix (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 MMLU items) and find that 31.3\\% of items exhibit large DIF (ETS Category~C, $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$) between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts, while a random-split sanity check yields 0.18\\%. We show that existing web-overlap contamination labels cannot validate item-level differential detectors (enrichment$\\approx$0.96 against Li et al.\\ labels), and propose a semi-synthetic injection framework with cross-subject matching ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000) for controlled validation. Complementary analyses---ConStat perturbation testing, DVD generation variance, ensemble fusion, Item-Focused Trees, Bayesian cumulative DIF, item generation calibration, and cross-benchmark replication on GSM8K---provide a comprehensive multi-method characterization. Graceful degradation analysis shows MMLU tolerates up to 50\\% item replacement ($\\rho=0.962$), suggesting instability does not threaten aggregate validity but reveals which capability dimensions shift across generations.\n\\end{abstract}\n\n\\section{Introduction}\n\nWhen we compare LLM benchmark scores across model generations, we implicitly assume \\emph{temporal comparability}: that each item measures the same construct with the same difficulty for all models. This assumption is rarely tested. Psychometrics offers Differential Item Functioning (DIF) analysis as a mature tool for exactly this purpose: DIF identifies items whose difficulty changes across groups after controlling for overall ability.\n\nWe apply the Mantel-Haenszel (MH) DIF procedure---a standard method in educational testing since \\citet{holland1988differential}---to the METABENCH response matrix comprising 5,227 models and 12,508 MMLU items. Our primary finding is that 31.3\\% of items are classified as ETS Category~C (large DIF, $|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$) between temporal cohorts, far exceeding the 0.18\\% rate from a random-split sanity check.\n\nWe emphasize that instability does not imply invalidity. MMLU rankings are massively redundant: removing all flagged items changes model rankings negligibly ($\\rho \\approx 0.997$). The DIF signal instead reveals \\emph{which} capability dimensions shift across model generations, serving as a diagnostic lens rather than a quality filter.\n\nBeyond this primary finding, we investigate whether existing contamination detection methods capture the same signal (they largely do not), propose a reusable semi-synthetic validation framework, and conduct extensive multi-method analyses including ConStat perturbation testing, DVD generation variance, ensemble fusion, Bayesian cumulative DIF, Item-Focused Trees, architecture stratification, item generation calibration, and cross-benchmark replication on GSM8K with GSM1K contamination gap validation.\n\n\\section{Related Work}\n\n\\paragraph{Benchmark contamination detection.} Several methods detect training data contamination in LLMs. Min-K\\% \\cite{shi2024detecting} uses token-level likelihood; ConStat \\cite{dekoninck2024constat} uses perturbation-based testing; n-gram overlap methods \\cite{li2024task} check web presence. These methods share a common validation methodology: evaluation against web-overlap labels as proxy ground truth.\n\n\\paragraph{Differential Item Functioning.} DIF analysis has a long history in educational measurement \\cite{holland1988differential,zumbo1999handbook}. The Mantel-Haenszel procedure \\cite{mantel1959statistical} tests for differential item difficulty between focal and reference groups conditional on total score. ETS uses a three-category classification system (A/B/C) based on effect size thresholds. Item-Focused Trees \\cite{tutz2016item} extend DIF to continuous covariates using recursive partitioning.\n\n\\paragraph{LLM benchmark evaluation.} Recent work questions the reliability of LLM benchmarks \\cite{polo2024tinybenchmarks,miller2024benchmark}. Growing Pains \\cite{ott2024growing} examines temporal degradation of benchmark utility. Our work connects psychometric measurement theory to NLP evaluation methodology.\n\n\\section{Method}\n\n\\subsection{MH-DIF Analysis}\n\nFor each item, we form a $2 \\times 2 \\times K$ contingency table stratified by total score deciles, comparing reference (2023 models) and focal (2024-H1 models) groups. The MH odds ratio $\\alpha_{\\text{MH}}$ is converted to the $\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}$ scale:\n\\begin{equation}\n\\Delta_{\\text{MH}} = -2.35 \\ln(\\alpha_{\\text{MH}})\n\\end{equation}\nItems are classified per ETS criteria: Category A ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| < 1.0$), B ($1.0 \\leq |\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| < 1.5$), and C ($|\\Delta_{\\text{MH}}| \\geq 1.5$). We report C\\% as our primary instability metric.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Injection Framework}\n\nWe inject controlled differential contamination into real response matrices by flipping incorrect$\\to$correct responses for a subset of items in the focal group with probability $p$. Detection power is measured as $\\Delta$TPR (change in true positive rate relative to uninjected baseline). Cross-subject matching uses total score on \\emph{other} subjects as the MH matching variable, eliminating matching variable contamination ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000).\n\n\\subsection{Complementary Methods}\n\n\\paragraph{ConStat perturbation.} We reformulate 220 MMLU items and test 45 models, measuring detection rate via the ConStat framework \\cite{dekoninck2024constat}.\n\n\\paragraph{DVD generation variance.} We generate 50 responses per item across 12 open-weight models, detecting bimodal response distributions indicative of memorization.\n\n\\paragraph{Ensemble fusion.} We combine DIF, ConStat, and DVD signals via majority voting and weighted fusion.\n\n\\paragraph{Item-Focused Trees.} Following \\citet{tutz2016item}, we fit item-focused trees with release date as a continuous covariate.\n\n\\paragraph{Bayesian cumulative DIF.} We simulate 4 benchmark administrations with Bayesian priors following \\citet{sinharay2006bayesian}.\n\n\\paragraph{Architecture stratification.} We restrict analysis to decoder-only models to test whether architecture heterogeneity drives IRT misfit.\n\n\\paragraph{Item generation.} We generate 300 replacement items for DIF-flagged questions using LLMs and calibrate via student simulation.\n\n\\section{Experiments}\n\n\\subsection{Primary DIF Analysis (MMLU)}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Cohort Design} & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{Notes} \\\\\n\\midrule\nTemporal (2023 vs 2024) & 31.3 & Primary \\\\\nAbility-matched & 2.9 & Near baseline \\\\\nRandom split (sanity) & 0.18 & $\\approx$0 \\\\\n\\midrule\nWithin-family: Llama-2 vs 3 & 61.3 & High capability gap \\\\\nWithin-family: Mistral/Mixtral & 38.7 & Moderate gap \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{DIF classification rates (ETS C\\%) by cohort design. Temporal cohorts show 31.3\\% large-DIF items; ability-matched cohorts drop to 2.9\\%; random splits show 0.18\\%. Within-family comparisons reveal higher rates for larger capability gaps.}\n\\label{tab:dif_cohorts}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:dif_cohorts} shows DIF rates across cohort designs. The temporal cohort yields 31.3\\% C-classified items. The ability-matched cohort drops to 2.9\\%, suggesting that much of the temporal signal reflects ability differences between model generations. The random-split sanity check at 0.18\\% confirms the signal is non-random.\n\nWithin-family comparisons are informative: Llama-2 vs.\\ Llama-3 (C\\%$=$61.3\\%) shows that even within the same model family, large capability jumps produce substantial DIF. Domain-level analysis reveals a weak positive correlation between DIF rate and domain accuracy improvement ($\\rho = 0.307$, $p = 0.02$).\n\n\\paragraph{IRT model fit.} We note that 98\\% of models show $|\\text{Lz}| > 2$ under unidimensional IRT, reflecting MMLU's inherent multidimensionality across 57 subjects. This misfit affects all IRT-derived statistics including MH-DIF.\n\n\\paragraph{Ground truth comparison.} Enrichment of DIF flags against Li et al.\\ web-overlap contamination labels is 0.96---near unity, indicating near-zero association. We argue this is expected: web-overlap labels measure global exposure, not differential contamination. Any item-level detector validated against these labels shares this limitation.\n\n\\subsection{RL Robustness}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Cohort} & \\textbf{C\\%} & \\textbf{MinK\\%} & \\textbf{ConStat} \\\\\n\\midrule\nSFT baseline & 30.8 & 0.61 & 0.58 \\\\\n+ Alignment RL & 31.1 & --- & --- \\\\\n+ Reasoning RL & 28.4 & 0.54 & 0.49 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{DIF flag stability and contamination detector AUROC across training paradigms ($N=13$ reasoning-RL models). Jaccard similarity: SFT vs alignment-RL = 0.72; SFT vs reasoning-RL = 0.58. DeLong $p=0.18$; TOST equivalence $p=0.09$.}\n\\label{tab:rl_robustness}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:rl_robustness} examines DIF stability when the model cohort includes RL-trained models. With only $N=13$ reasoning-RL models available, results are suggestive rather than definitive. C\\% remains stable (28.4--31.1\\%), and the Jaccard drop from 0.72 (alignment-RL) to 0.58 (reasoning-RL) indicates some flag instability. Traditional detectors show modest degradation (MinK\\% AUROC: $0.61 \\to 0.54$; ConStat: $0.58 \\to 0.49$). The DeLong test is non-significant ($p=0.18$), and TOST equivalence does not reach significance ($p=0.09$).\n\n\\subsection{ConStat Perturbation Comparison}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{ConStat} & \\textbf{DIF} \\\\\n\\midrule\nDetection rate & 24\\% (18\\% FDR-corr.) & 31.3\\% \\\\\nPrecision vs Li et al. & 0.42 & $\\approx$enrichment 0.96 \\\\\nRecall vs Li et al. & 0.31 & --- \\\\\nJaccard overlap & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{0.09} \\\\\nEffect size (orig. vs reform.) & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{0.34} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{ConStat perturbation testing on 220 MMLU items across 45 models vs.\\ DIF. Near-zero Jaccard overlap (0.09) suggests the methods measure different constructs.}\n\\label{tab:constat}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:constat} compares ConStat and DIF. ConStat detects contamination at 24\\% pre-FDR (18\\% post-correction) on the 220 reformulated items. The near-zero Jaccard overlap with DIF flags (0.09) indicates these methods capture fundamentally different signals. ConStat shows higher precision (0.42) against Li et al.\\ labels than DIF's enrichment$\\approx$0.96 (near-chance) association.\n\n\\subsection{DVD Generation Variance}\n\nAcross 12 models and 500 items (50 generations each), DVD detects bimodal response distributions in only 7\\% of items. Overlap with DIF is minimal (Jaccard$=$0.05) and with ConStat slightly higher (Jaccard$=$0.11). DVD AUROC against Li et al.\\ labels is 0.56. Results vary $>$15\\% between temperature settings ($T=0.7$ vs $T=1.0$), raising protocol sensitivity concerns. The analysis required 68 GPU-hours.\n\n\\subsection{Ensemble Fusion}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Fusion Strategy} & \\textbf{Precision} & \\textbf{Recall / AUROC} \\\\\n\\midrule\nMajority voting & 0.15 & Recall: 0.04 \\\\\nWeighted fusion & --- & AUROC: 0.59 \\\\\nUniform weights & --- & AUROC: 0.56 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Ensemble fusion of DIF + ConStat + DVD. All strategies perform near chance. Only 28 items flagged by all three methods. Cross-validation variance: 0.08; optimized vs uniform $\\Delta$AUROC: 0.03.}\n\\label{tab:ensemble}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:ensemble} presents ensemble fusion results. Majority voting yields extremely low recall (0.04) because few items are flagged by multiple methods. Weighted fusion AUROC (0.59) barely exceeds chance, with high cross-validation variance (0.08) indicating unstable weights. Only 28 items are flagged by all three methods. The optimized weights improve over uniform by only $\\Delta$AUROC$=$0.03, suggesting no meaningful signal structure in the combination.\n\n\\subsection{Cross-Benchmark: GSM8K and GSM1K}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Metric} & \\textbf{Value} & \\textbf{95\\% CI} \\\\\n\\midrule\nGSM8K temporal C\\% & 18.9\\% & --- \\\\\nSemi-synthetic $\\Delta$TPR ($p{=}0.10$) & 0.818 & --- \\\\\n\\midrule\n\\multicolumn{3}{l}{\\textit{GSM1K contamination gap ($N=33$ models)}} \\\\\nPearson $r$ & $-0.478$ & [$-0.714$, $-0.155$] \\\\\nSpearman $\\rho$ & $-0.403$ & [$-0.687$, $-0.031$] \\\\\nPartial $r$ (ctrl.\\ accuracy) & $-0.385$ & [$-0.640$, $-0.058$] \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{GSM8K DIF replication and GSM1K contamination gap correlation. DIF-flagged items anti-correlate with contamination-based gains. Max Cook's D: Deepseek-math-7b-rl. Leave-one-out $r$: [$-0.537$, $-0.374$].}\n\\label{tab:gsm}\n\\end{table}\n\nGSM8K (1,319 items) shows 18.9\\% temporal C\\%, lower than MMLU but consistent in direction. The semi-synthetic framework replicates with $\\Delta$TPR$=$0.818 at $p=0.10$ injection. Table~\\ref{tab:gsm} presents the GSM1K correlation: DIF-flagged items anti-correlate with contamination gaps ($r = -0.478$, $p = 0.005$; $N=33$ models). Confidence intervals are wide (upper bound $|r| = 0.155$ for Pearson), and the max Cook's D model is Deepseek-math-7b-rl.\n\n\\subsection{Bifactor IRT Equating}\n\nThe bifactor model (1 general + 57 subject factors, 12,508 items) failed to converge after 48 hours of estimation. Fit indices were poor (RMSEA~$> 0.10$; CFI~$< 0.80$), and no anchor items could be identified for equating. This confirms that unidimensional IRT is fundamentally misspecified for MMLU, a limitation that affects all our MH-DIF analyses.\n\n\\subsection{Item Generation Calibration}\n\nWe generated 300 replacement items for DIF-flagged questions. Prompt-based difficulty targeting achieved $\\rho = 0.18$ with original items; student simulation improved this to $\\rho = 0.52$, but both fall below the $r = 0.75$--$0.82$ range reported in prior work. Construct coverage was not maintained: generated items cluster in easily-generatable knowledge areas. Holdout validation ($\\rho = 0.72$) is below the 0.95 target threshold. Expert quality rating averaged 2.8/5.\n\n\\subsection{Bayesian Cumulative DIF}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Administrations} & \\textbf{Power ($\\delta{=}1.0$)} & \\textbf{Power ($\\delta{=}1.4$)} \\\\\n\\midrule\n1 & 0.38 & --- \\\\\n2 (cumulative) & 0.49 & --- \\\\\n3 (cumulative) & 0.58 & --- \\\\\n4 (cumulative) & 0.64 & 0.73 \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Bayesian cumulative DIF power across simulated administrations. Medium-effect power reaches 0.64 after 4 administrations, below the 0.70 target. All results are simulation-only.}\n\\label{tab:bayesian}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:bayesian} shows power gains from Bayesian prior accumulation across simulated administrations. For medium effects ($\\delta=1.0$), cumulative power reaches 0.64 after 4 administrations---below the 0.70 target. For large effects ($\\delta=1.4$), 4-administration power is 0.73. These results are entirely simulation-based with no empirical validation, as multi-wave benchmark data does not exist.\n\n\\subsection{Graceful Degradation}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Replacement Rate} & $\\rho$ & \\textbf{Reliability Drop} \\\\\n\\midrule\n5\\% & 0.998 & --- \\\\\n10\\% & 0.995 & --- \\\\\n20\\% & 0.988 & --- \\\\\n31\\% (DIF rate) & --- & 0.006 \\\\\n50\\% & 0.962 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Ranking stability ($\\rho$) under progressive random item replacement. MMLU tolerates substantial replacement with minimal ranking disruption. At 31\\% replacement (matching our DIF rate), reliability drops by only 0.006.}\n\\label{tab:degradation}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:degradation} demonstrates that MMLU rankings are highly robust to item replacement. Even at 50\\% replacement, $\\rho = 0.962$. At the 31\\% level matching our DIF finding, reliability drops by only 0.006. Four of 57 subjects lose $>$50\\% of items at 31\\% replacement, but these are small subjects ($N < 50$ items). The analytical lower bound on validity is 0.94.\n\n\\subsection{Item-Focused Trees}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lcc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Split Variable} & \\textbf{\\% Items} & \\textbf{Importance} \\\\\n\\midrule\nRelease date & 27.4\\% & Primary \\\\\nModel size & --- & 19\\% (secondary) \\\\\nTraining cutoff & --- & 12\\% (secondary) \\\\\n\\midrule\nMH concordance & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{$\\kappa = 0.61$} \\\\\nCV AUC drop & \\multicolumn{2}{c}{0.14 (overfitting)} \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Item-Focused Tree split analysis. Release date is the primary split variable for 27.4\\% of items with significant DIF, consistent with temporal MH-DIF. Moderate concordance with MH (0.61).}\n\\label{tab:ift}\n\\end{table}\n\nItem-Focused Trees (Table~\\ref{tab:ift}) identify release date as the primary split for 27.4\\% of items with significant splits---broadly consistent with temporal MH-DIF. Concordance with MH classifications is moderate (0.61). Secondary splits on model size (19\\%) and training data cutoff (12\\%) suggest DIF is partially associated with these covariates. The cross-validation AUC drop of 0.14 indicates some overfitting.\n\n\\subsection{Architecture Stratification}\n\nMETABENCH MMLU comprises 99.98\\% decoder-only models (5,226/5,227). Restricting to decoder-only models produces identical DIF results: C\\%$=$31.3\\% (Jaccard$=$1.0 with full analysis). IRT RMSEA improves negligibly ($0.091 \\to 0.089$), and misfit remains at 97.8\\%. Architecture heterogeneity is not a source of IRT misfit; MMLU's 57-subject multidimensionality is.\n\n\\subsection{Semi-Synthetic Validation}\n\n\\begin{table}[t]\n\\centering\n\\small\n\\begin{tabular}{lccc}\n\\toprule\n\\textbf{Injection $p$} & \\textbf{$\\Delta$TPR} & \\textbf{FPR (std)} & \\textbf{FPR (ext)} \\\\\n\\midrule\n0.00 (baseline) & --- & 0.298 & 0.314 \\\\\n0.03 & +0.548 & --- & --- \\\\\n0.05 & +0.508 & --- & --- \\\\\n0.07 & +0.990 & --- & --- \\\\\n0.10 & +0.674 & --- & --- \\\\\n1.00 & --- & 0.616 & --- \\\\\n\\bottomrule\n\\end{tabular}\n\\caption{Semi-synthetic injection results on 500 clean items with 200 injected. Clear dose-response in $\\Delta$TPR. Baseline FPR is 0.314 (31.4\\% of clean items flagged). FPR inflation under standard matching rises to 0.616 at full injection.}\n\\label{tab:semisyn}\n\\end{table}\n\nTable~\\ref{tab:semisyn} presents semi-synthetic validation results. The dose-response curve shows monotonically increasing $\\Delta$TPR from $p=0.03$ to $p=0.07$, confirming DIF sensitivity to injected contamination. However, the baseline FPR of 0.314 (31.4\\% of ``clean'' items flagged before any injection) indicates a high base rate. Standard MH matching suffers FPR inflation under contamination ($0.298 \\to 0.616$ at $p=1.0$) because the matching variable is contaminated. Cross-subject matching eliminates this issue ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000).\n\n\\section{Discussion}\n\n\\subsection{Synthesis}\n\nOur multi-method investigation reveals several key findings. First, 31.3\\% of MMLU items show large temporal DIF, far above the 0.18\\% random baseline. This finding is robust to architecture composition (99.98\\% decoder-only) but sensitive to cohort design: ability-matched cohorts show only 2.9\\% C\\%, suggesting ability differences between generations drive much of the signal.\n\nSecond, the three detection methods---DIF, ConStat, and DVD---capture largely non-overlapping item sets (pairwise Jaccard $\\leq 0.11$). Their ensemble performs near chance (AUROC$=$0.59), indicating they measure fundamentally different constructs rather than converging on a common contamination signal.\n\nThird, MMLU tolerates substantial item instability ($\\rho = 0.962$ at 50\\% replacement), suggesting the 31\\% finding, while statistically robust, may have limited practical impact on benchmark validity.\n\nFourth, cross-benchmark replication on GSM8K (C\\%$=$18.9\\%) confirms the pattern generalizes, and the negative correlation with GSM1K contamination gaps ($r = -0.478$) suggests DIF items capture genuine capability differences rather than contamination artifacts.\n\n\\subsection{Limitations}\n\n\\paragraph{IRT misspecification.} The most fundamental limitation is IRT model misfit: 98\\% of models show $|\\text{Lz}| > 2$, and the bifactor model failed to converge. MH-DIF conditions on a unidimensional total score, which is misspecified for a 57-subject benchmark. Multidimensional IRT approaches may yield different results.\n\n\\paragraph{Ability confounding.} The drop from 31.3\\% (temporal) to 2.9\\% (ability-matched) C\\% suggests substantial ability confounding. While we argue this reflects genuine item-level changes, the alternative explanation---that DIF primarily detects capability improvement---cannot be fully excluded.\n\n\\paragraph{Sample sizes.} The RL robustness analysis uses only $N=13$ reasoning-RL models. The GSM1K validation uses $N=33$ models with wide confidence intervals. Both are underpowered.\n\n\\paragraph{Semi-synthetic mechanism.} Our injection mechanism (incorrect$\\to$correct flipping) simplifies real contamination processes. The 31.4\\% baseline FPR on ``clean'' items raises questions about what DIF measures absent intentional contamination.\n\n\\paragraph{Simulation-only results.} The Bayesian cumulative DIF analysis is entirely simulated. Item generation calibration falls below practical thresholds ($\\rho = 0.52$; expert rating 2.8/5).\n\n\\paragraph{Method disagreement.} The near-zero overlap between DIF, ConStat, and DVD (Jaccard $\\leq 0.11$) and failed ensemble (AUROC$=$0.59) raise the possibility that none of these methods reliably measures contamination, or that ``contamination'' is not a unitary construct amenable to item-level detection.\n\n\\section{Conclusion}\n\nWe applied MH-DIF to the largest LLM benchmark response matrix analyzed to date (5,227 models $\\times$ 12,508 items) and found that 31.3\\% of MMLU items are classified as having large DIF between 2023 and 2024 model cohorts. This finding is robust to random-split controls (0.18\\%) and threshold variation (10--49\\% across standard cutoffs), but substantially reduced when controlling for ability differences (2.9\\%). The three contamination detection paradigms we compared---psychometric DIF, perturbation-based ConStat, and generation variance DVD---flag largely disjoint item sets, and their ensemble does not improve over individual methods. MMLU's graceful degradation under item replacement ($\\rho > 0.96$ at 50\\%) suggests these instabilities do not compromise aggregate ranking validity. We release our semi-synthetic validation framework, including the cross-subject matching procedure ($\\Delta$FPR$=$0.000), as a reusable tool for evaluating item-level benchmark analyses.\n\n\\bibliography{references}\n\\bibliographystyle{acl_natbib}\n\n\\end{document}\n","structuredPatch":[],"originalFile":null,"userModified":false}}
